{"confidence": 0.3380073606967926, "correct": false, "expected": "pay_subject_to_15000_sublimit", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-01", "item_sha256": "d5b54287fc861db48da82bf3731ba24e708debafe88cceb156d5fe90513ce5d2", "latency_ms": 721.9440396875143, "options": ["deny_vacancy_exclusion", "deny_repeated_seepage", "pay_subject_to_10000_sublimit", "pay_subject_to_15000_sublimit", "pay_full_estimate_less_deductible"], "prediction": "deny_vacancy_exclusion", "probabilities": {"deny_repeated_seepage": 0.05873686820268631, "deny_vacancy_exclusion": 0.3380073606967926, "pay_full_estimate_less_deductible": 0.12434597313404083, "pay_subject_to_10000_sublimit": 0.14090242981910706, "pay_subject_to_15000_sublimit": 0.3380073606967926}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "HARBORLINE MUTUAL INSURANCE COMPANY\nHOMEOWNERS POLICY FORM HM-3 (EDITION 04/2023) \u2014 EXCERPTS, ENDORSEMENTS AND CLAIM FILE HX-2026-118804\nPrepared for coverage review. Page numbers omitted. Bracketed notes are from the file clerk.\n\n======================================================================\nPART A \u2014 DECLARATIONS (CURRENT TERM)\n======================================================================\nNamed insured: Dana Okafor\nResidence premises: 14 Linden Crest Road, Millbrook\nPolicy number: HM3-7730-2291-04\nOriginal inception: March 15, 2019\nCurrent policy period: March 15, 2026 to March 15, 2027 (12:01 a.m. standard time at the residence premises). This term was issued as a renewal of the prior term March 15, 2025 to March 15, 2026.\nCoverage A \u2014 Dwelling: $418,000\nCoverage B \u2014 Other Structures: $41,800\nCoverage C \u2014 Personal Property: $209,000\nCoverage D \u2014 Loss of Use: $83,600\nSection II \u2014 Personal Liability: $300,000 each occurrence; Medical Payments: $5,000 each person\nAll-peril deductible: $1,000\nWind/hail deductible: 2% of Coverage A\nForms and endorsements attached to this term: HM-3 (04/23); HE-0110 (Special Provisions \u2014 Millbrook County); HE-1840 (Limited Fungi, Wet or Dry Rot, or Bacteria Coverage \u2014 $5,000); HE-2291 (Concealed Water Sublimit Revision); HE-3007 (Scheduled Personal Property \u2014 jewelry, 2 items); HE-4412 (Water Backup of Sewers or Drains \u2014 $10,000).\nMortgagee: Tri-County Savings, loan no. 00-448120\nPremium: $2,184.00 annual; paid in full.\n\n======================================================================\nPART B \u2014 POLICY FORM HM-3 (04/2023), SELECTED PROVISIONS\n======================================================================\nDEFINITIONS\nIn this policy, \"you\" and \"your\" refer to the named insured shown in the Declarations and the spouse if a resident of the same household. \"We\", \"us\" and \"our\" refer to the company providing this insurance.\n\n1. \"Bodily injury\" means bodily harm, sickness or disease, including required care, loss of services and death that results.\n2. \"Business\" means a trade, profession or occupation engaged in on a full-time, part-time or occasional basis, or any other activity engaged in for money or other compensation, subject to the exceptions in Section II.\n3. \"Concealed water\" means water or steam that escapes from within a plumbing, heating, air conditioning or automatic fire protective sprinkler system, or from within a household appliance, and that remains hidden within the walls, floors or ceilings of the dwelling, where the presence of such water was not known to and could not reasonably have been known to an \"insured\" before the damage became visible.\n4. \"Insured\" means you and residents of your household who are your relatives, or other persons under the age of 21 and in the care of any person named above.\n5. \"Occurrence\" means an accident, including continuous or repeated exposure to substantially the same general harmful conditions, which results during the policy period in bodily injury or property damage.\n6. \"Repeated seepage or leakage\" means continuous or repeated seepage or leakage of water or steam over a period of 14 or more days.\n7. \"Residence premises\" means the one-family dwelling where you reside and which is shown as the residence premises in the Declarations.\n8. \"Unoccupied\" means the dwelling contains contents, including furnishings and personal property, sufficient to support customary occupancy, but no person is living in it.\n9. \"Vacant\" means the dwelling lacks contents, including furnishings and personal property, sufficient to support customary occupancy, whether or not a person is present. A dwelling under construction is not considered vacant.\n\nSECTION I \u2014 PROPERTY COVERAGES\nCoverage A \u2014 Dwelling. We cover the dwelling on the residence premises, including structures attached to the dwelling, and materials and supplies located on or next to the residence premises used to construct, alter or repair the dwelling.\nCoverage B \u2014 Other Structures. We cover other structures on the residence premises set apart from the dwelling by clear space. Structures connected to the dwelling by only a fence, utility line or similar connection are considered other structures.\nCoverage C \u2014 Personal Property. We cover personal property owned or used by an insured while it is anywhere in the world. Special limits apply to money ($200), securities ($1,500), watercraft ($1,500), trailers ($1,500), theft of jewelry ($1,500), theft of firearms ($2,500), and theft of silverware ($2,500).\nCoverage D \u2014 Loss of Use. If a loss covered under Section I makes the residence premises not fit to live in, we cover any necessary increase in living expenses incurred by you so that your household can maintain its normal standard of living.\n\nSECTION I \u2014 PERILS INSURED AGAINST\nWe insure against direct physical loss to property described in Coverages A and B, except as limited or excluded in this policy.\nAccidental discharge or overflow of water or steam from within a plumbing, heating, air conditioning or automatic fire protective sprinkler system or from within a household appliance is covered, subject to the exclusions and limits below. We also pay the reasonable cost of tearing out and replacing any part of a building necessary to repair the system or appliance from which the water or steam escaped; we do not pay for the system or appliance itself.\n\nSECTION I \u2014 EXCLUSIONS\n4.1 Earth Movement. Earthquake, landslide, mudflow, subsidence or sinkhole, unless direct loss by fire or explosion ensues.\n4.2 Freezing. Freezing of a plumbing, heating, air conditioning or sprinkler system or a household appliance while the dwelling is vacant or unoccupied, unless you have used reasonable care to maintain heat in the building or to shut off the water supply and drain all systems and appliances.\n4.3 Repeated Seepage or Leakage. We do not cover loss caused by \"repeated seepage or leakage\" of water or steam.\n    4.3.1 Exception for concealed water. Exclusion 4.3 does not apply to loss caused by \"concealed water\". Our total payment for all loss caused by concealed water in any one occurrence will not exceed $10,000, including the cost of tearing out and replacing any part of the building. This limit does not increase the Coverage A limit.\n4.4 Wear and Tear. Wear and tear, marring, deterioration; mechanical breakdown, latent defect, inherent vice; smog, rust or other corrosion. However, if one of these causes the accidental discharge of water or steam, we cover the resulting water damage to the building, subject to 4.3 and 4.7; we do not cover the corroded pipe itself.\n4.5 Neglect. Neglect of an insured to use all reasonable means to save and preserve property at and after the time of a loss.\n4.6 Ordinance or Law. Enforcement of any ordinance or law regulating construction, repair or demolition, except as provided under Additional Coverages.\n4.7 Vacancy. We do not cover loss caused by vandalism, malicious mischief, glass breakage, or any accidental discharge or overflow of water or steam, if the dwelling has been vacant for more than 60 consecutive days immediately before the loss.\n4.8 Intentional Loss. Any loss arising out of any act an insured commits or conspires to commit with the intent to cause a loss.\n\nSECTION I \u2014 CONDITIONS\nDuties After Loss. In case of a loss to covered property, you must see that the following are done: give prompt notice to us or our agent; protect the property from further damage and make reasonable and necessary repairs to protect it; keep an accurate record of repair expenses; prepare an inventory of damaged personal property; as often as we reasonably require, show the damaged property and submit to examination under oath; send us within 60 days after our request your signed, sworn proof of loss.\nLoss Settlement. Buildings under Coverage A are settled at replacement cost without deduction for depreciation, subject to the limits and sublimits of this policy, provided you actually repair or replace the damaged building within 180 days after the loss.\nDeductible. Unless otherwise noted in this policy, the following deductible provision applies: subject to the policy limits that apply, we will pay only that part of the total of all loss payable under Section I that exceeds the deductible amount shown in the Declarations. Where a sublimit applies, the deductible is applied to the loss before the sublimit is applied.\nMortgage Clause. If a mortgagee is named in this policy, any loss payable under Coverage A or B will be paid to the mortgagee and you, as interests appear.\n\n======================================================================\nPART C \u2014 ENDORSEMENTS\n======================================================================\nHE-0110 SPECIAL PROVISIONS \u2014 MILLBROOK COUNTY\nCancellation notices for nonpayment of premium must be mailed at least 20 days before the cancellation date. Any claim settlement dispute may be submitted to the Millbrook County appraisal panel under the Appraisal condition. Nothing in this endorsement changes any coverage limit or exclusion.\n\nHE-1840 LIMITED FUNGI, WET OR DRY ROT, OR BACTERIA COVERAGE\nWe will pay up to $5,000 in the aggregate during the policy period for direct physical loss caused by fungi, wet or dry rot or bacteria that result from a covered peril, including the cost of testing and remediation. This limit is separate from and in addition to any water-damage limit or sublimit.\n\nHE-2291 CONCEALED WATER SUBLIMIT REVISION (EDITION 11/2025)\nThis endorsement modifies insurance provided under Form HM-3.\nA. Applicability. This endorsement applies only to policies issued, or renewed, on or after January 1, 2026. For policies whose current term was issued or renewed before that date, Exception 4.3.1 continues to apply as written in the form.\nB. In Exception 4.3.1, the amount \"$10,000\" is replaced by \"$15,000\".\nC. All other terms of Exclusion 4.3 and of the definition of \"concealed water\" remain unchanged. This endorsement does not modify Exclusion 4.7 (Vacancy).\n\nHE-3007 SCHEDULED PERSONAL PROPERTY\nItem 1: Ladies' platinum ring with 1.1 ct diamond \u2014 $6,400. Item 2: Men's gold wristwatch \u2014 $3,150. Coverage applies worldwide; no deductible applies to scheduled items.\n\nHE-4412 WATER BACKUP OF SEWERS OR DRAINS\nWe cover direct physical loss caused by water which backs up through sewers or drains or overflows from a sump, up to $10,000 per occurrence. This coverage does not apply to water that escapes from a plumbing supply line.\n\n======================================================================\nPART D \u2014 CLAIM FILE HX-2026-118804\n======================================================================\nFNOL (first notice of loss), recorded by phone agent K. Mendes, August 5, 2026, 10:42:\nInsured reports water staining and soft drywall on the hallway side of the upstairs bathroom and a warped cabinet base under the vanity. Insured states she returned home on August 3, 2026 in the evening after an extended stay out of state and noticed the stain the next morning. Water supply shut off at the main by insured on August 4. No active dripping visible. Insured requests adjuster visit. No injuries. No prior water claims on this policy.\n\nAdjuster field notes \u2014 R. Bhattacharya, August 7, 2026:\n- Two-storey colonial, built 1987. Upstairs hall bath shares a wall with the hallway linen closet.\n- Drywall removed by plumber at my request on the vanity wall. Found a pinhole failure in the 1/2-inch copper cold supply line, approximately 30 inches above the floor inside the wall cavity. Heavy green-blue corrosion around the pinhole; mineral trail running down the stud bay; subfloor darkened and delaminated over an area roughly 3 ft x 4 ft; ceiling below (kitchen) shows a faint ring but no sag.\n- Moisture meter readings in the wall bay 38\u201346% (saturated) on day 1; kitchen ceiling 19%.\n- No visible signs of water on the finished surfaces were reported by anyone before August 4. Insured states she had no reason to suspect a leak; neighbour who collected mail weekly did not enter the upstairs bathroom.\n- House fully furnished; beds made; refrigerator stocked with condiments and frozen goods; clothing in closets; utilities on; thermostat set to 78\u00b0F cooling. No signs of forced entry.\n- Insured was away from May 20, 2026 to August 3, 2026 caring for her brother after surgery. No one stayed at the house during that period.\n\nPlumber's report \u2014 Millbrook Plumbing & Heating, invoice 55821, August 7, 2026:\n\"Pinhole leak in type M copper supply. Corrosion and mineral build-up at the failure indicate the leak had been active for a considerable time \u2014 in our opinion at least four and most likely five to six weeks. Line cut out and replaced with PEX, 4 ft. Pressure tested OK. Charge $640.00 (repair of the supply line itself).\"\n\nContractor estimate \u2014 Ridgeway Restoration, estimate RR-2026-3310, August 12, 2026 (building repairs only; plumbing repair excluded):\n  Demolition and debris removal ........................ $1,850\n  Structural drying (6 days, 4 air movers, 1 dehumidifier)  $2,760\n  Subfloor replacement, 12 sq ft + joist sistering ...... $3,420\n  Drywall and finishing, bath and hall walls ............ $2,980\n  Vanity cabinet replacement (like kind and quality) .... $3,150\n  Tile repair, bathroom floor ........................... $4,190\n  Kitchen ceiling stain treatment and repaint ........... $1,120\n  Painting, bath and hall ............................... $1,630\n  Contractor overhead and profit ........................ $1,300\n  TOTAL ............................................... $22,400\nMould testing performed August 8: no fungal growth above background; no remediation required.\n\nInternal note \u2014 trainee adjuster J. Pike, August 13, 2026:\n\"Insured was gone 75 days, which is over the 60-day vacancy limit, so 4.7 should knock out the water damage. Recommend denial letter. Please confirm.\"\n\nInternal note \u2014 file clerk, August 14, 2026:\nPolicy history shows renewal offer mailed January 30, 2026; renewal premium paid February 26, 2026; renewal term effective March 15, 2026. Prior term (2025\u20132026) was issued on the 2023 form without HE-2291. [HE-2291 appears on the current-term Declarations.]\n\nInternal note \u2014 coverage unit intake, August 14, 2026:\nContents claim: none presented (vanity contents and towels discarded by insured, value under $100, insured declined to claim). Additional living expense: none (bathroom downstairs usable). Mortgagee to be named on any building payment.\n\nNeighbour statement (by phone, August 10, 2026): Resident at 12 Linden Crest Road confirms she collected mail and checked the front door weekly on Saturdays while the insured was away, and watered potted plants on the porch. She did not go upstairs. She noticed nothing unusual.\n\nWeather data (for completeness): no freezing temperatures at the premises between May and August 2026; no named storms; one thunderstorm on July 18, 2026 with no reported roof or window damage.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.7793393731117249, "correct": false, "expected": "cfo", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-04", "item_sha256": "cb096df4ce0053caaa2faa4019ef99b35a6124583888e36850f1787d6ac5acc9", "latency_ms": 456.2965175136924, "options": ["budget_holder", "department_head", "vp_and_finance_director", "cfo", "executive_committee"], "prediction": "department_head", "probabilities": {"budget_holder": 0.09307881444692612, "cfo": 0.018328305333852768, "department_head": 0.7793393731117249, "executive_committee": 0.016174672171473503, "vp_and_finance_director": 0.09307881444692612}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "CASTELLAN FOODS GMBH \u2014 GROUP PROCUREMENT POLICY GP-01 (VERSION 7, EFFECTIVE 1 MARCH 2025)\nEXTRACT FOR APPROVAL ROUTING, WITH AMENDMENT GP-01/A3 AND PURCHASE REQUEST PR-2026-4471\n\nPART I \u2014 POLICY EXTRACT\n\n1. Purpose and scope\n1.1 This policy governs the purchase of all goods and services by Castellan Foods GmbH and its subsidiaries, excluding raw agricultural commodities bought on the commodity desk, intercompany charges, taxes and levies, payroll, and payments to public authorities.\n1.2 The objectives are value for money, fair treatment of suppliers, segregation of duties and compliance with the Group's Code of Conduct and the Supplier Code.\n\n2. Definitions\n2.1 \"Requester\" \u2014 the employee who raises a purchase request in the e-procurement system (ProcureHub).\n2.2 \"Budget Holder\" \u2014 the manager accountable for the cost centre that will bear the cost.\n2.3 \"Total Contract Value\" or \"TCV\" \u2014 the total amount the Group may become obliged to pay the supplier under the contract over its maximum possible duration, including (a) all recurring fees for the initial term, (b) all recurring fees for any renewal or extension periods that the Group can trigger unilaterally or that occur automatically unless cancelled, (c) one-time fees such as set-up, implementation, onboarding, training or migration fees, and (d) any committed minimum volumes. TCV excludes VAT and other recoverable indirect taxes. Where fees are not fixed for renewal periods, the fee for the last year of the initial term is used.\n2.4 Discounts. A discount reduces TCV only if it is unconditional and written into the order form or contract to be signed. Conditional, time-limited or verbal offers (for example \"5% off if signed by the end of the month\") are disregarded when calculating TCV, even if the Requester intends to meet the condition.\n2.5 \"Related Purchase\" \u2014 a purchase from the same supplier (including its affiliates) for the same or a directly related business purpose.\n2.6 \"Sole-source\" \u2014 a purchase where fewer than three compliant quotes were obtained without an approved sourcing waiver.\n\n3. Competitive quotes\n3.1 Purchases with TCV above EUR 25,000 require at least three written quotes or an approved sourcing waiver.\n3.2 Framework agreements listed in Annex C have already been competitively tendered; call-offs under them need no further quotes.\n\n4. Approval thresholds (by TCV)\nLevel 1 \u2014 up to and including EUR 10,000: Budget Holder.\nLevel 2 \u2014 above EUR 10,000 up to and including EUR 50,000: Department Head.\nLevel 3 \u2014 above EUR 50,000 up to and including EUR 150,000: Vice President of the requesting function jointly with the Group Finance Director.\nLevel 4 \u2014 above EUR 150,000 up to and including EUR 500,000: Chief Financial Officer.\nLevel 5 \u2014 above EUR 500,000: Executive Committee.\nEach level includes the approvals of the levels below it in the workflow; the \"required approval level\" is the highest level the purchase reaches.\n\n5. Anti-splitting\n5.1 Requests must not be split to avoid a higher approval level.\n5.2 Aggregation rule. When determining the approval level, the TCV of a request is added to the TCV of all Related Purchases approved in the 90 calendar days before the date the new request is submitted. The combined figure determines the required approval level for the new request. This applies whether or not the earlier purchase was deliberately split.\n\n6. Additional reviews (independent of approval level)\n6.1 Information Security review for any software, SaaS, hosting or data-processing service.\n6.2 Legal review for contracts with non-standard liability clauses or a term longer than 36 months.\n6.3 Sustainability screening for suppliers of packaging and logistics services.\n\n7. Currency\nAmounts in other currencies are converted at the Group treasury rate published on the day of submission.\n\n8. Supplier onboarding\n8.1 New suppliers must be created in the supplier master by the Supplier Master Data team before a purchase order can be issued. The Requester provides the supplier's legal name, registered address, VAT number, bank details on supplier letterhead, and a signed acknowledgement of the Supplier Code.\n8.2 Bank details are verified by a call-back to a phone number obtained independently of the documents supplied. Changes to supplier bank details follow the same procedure.\n8.3 Suppliers located outside the EU are screened against the sanctions lists before creation and monthly thereafter.\n\n9. Purchase orders and invoices\n9.1 No commitment may be made to a supplier before the purchase order is issued (\"no PO, no pay\"). Retroactive purchase orders are reported to the Audit Committee quarterly.\n9.2 Invoices are matched three ways (purchase order, goods receipt or service confirmation, invoice). Price variances above 2% or EUR 500, whichever is lower, are blocked for Budget Holder review.\n9.3 Standard payment terms are 60 days end of month; shorter terms require Group Treasury approval unless the supplier is a small enterprise as defined by the Group's SME policy.\n\n10. Exceptions and breaches\n10.1 Any purchase that did not follow this policy is recorded as a policy breach by Procurement Compliance, with the approval level that would have been required.\n10.2 Repeated breaches by the same Requester are escalated to the Requester's VP and to Internal Audit.\n10.3 Approval by a person at a lower level than required is not valid and does not bind the Group internally; the request must be re-routed.\n\nANNEX C \u2014 FRAMEWORK AGREEMENTS (EXTRACT)\nFA-11 ForeLogix Consulting GmbH \u2014 supply-chain consulting day rates (valid to 31 Dec 2027)\nFA-14 Nordmark Office Supplies \u2014 stationery and office consumables\nFA-19 Brightwave Staffing \u2014 temporary warehouse staff\nFA-22 Teller & Sons \u2014 pallet repair and pallet pooling\n\nPART II \u2014 AMENDMENT GP-01/A3 (APPROVED BY THE CFO ON 15 JANUARY 2026, EFFECTIVE 1 FEBRUARY 2026)\nA3.1 In clause 4, the Level 3 and Level 4 thresholds are unchanged. The Level 2 upper limit is unchanged.\nA3.2 Clause 5.2 is amended by replacing \"60 calendar days\" with \"90 calendar days\". [Clerk note: the consolidated text above already shows 90 days.]\nA3.3 New clause 3.3: \"A sourcing waiver may be granted by the Head of Procurement for purchases with TCV up to EUR 150,000.\"\nA3.4 For SaaS subscriptions with automatic renewal, the Requester must record the cancellation notice period in ProcureHub.\n\nPART III \u2014 PURCHASE REQUEST PR-2026-4471\n\nSubmitted in ProcureHub: 30 August 2026\nRequester: Hanne Voss, Demand Planning Lead\nBudget Holder: Jonas Reiter, Head of Supply Chain Planning (cost centre 4120)\nFunction VP: Dr. Miriam Kalb, VP Operations\nSupplier: Forelight Analytics B.V. (Rotterdam)\nDescription: Forelight Demand Cloud \u2014 SaaS demand-forecasting platform for the chilled and frozen categories, including implementation and user training.\nSourcing: three quotes obtained (Forelight Analytics B.V., Quantiva Planning Ltd, Demandly SAS); Forelight scored highest on the evaluation matrix (attached).\n\nCommercial terms from Forelight order form draft v3 (to be signed):\n- Initial subscription term: 12 months from go-live, annual fee EUR 41,500 (excl. VAT), invoiced annually in advance.\n- Renewal: the subscription renews automatically for up to two further 12-month periods at the same annual fee unless Castellan gives 60 days' written notice before the end of the then-current term.\n- Implementation services (fixed fee): EUR 9,800.\n- End-user training (fixed fee, two sessions): EUR 2,400.\n- Payment terms: 30 days net.\n- Liability cap: 12 months' fees (standard template).\n- Data processing agreement: Forelight standard DPA, EU hosting (Frankfurt).\n\nRequester's comments in ProcureHub:\n\"Annual cost is only EUR 41.5k, which is under the Department Head limit. Forelight's account manager also confirmed by email on 22 August that we get 5% off the first-year fee if we sign by 30 September \u2014 we will definitely sign before then, so the real first-year fee is EUR 39,425. Please route to Jonas for approval so we can start in October.\"\n\nEmail from Forelight account manager, 22 August 2026 (attached):\n\"As discussed, if Castellan signs the order form by 30 September 2026 we will apply a 5% promotional discount to the first-year subscription fee. This offer is not reflected in the order form and will be applied as a credit note after signature.\"\n\nProcureHub history \u2014 supplier Forelight Analytics B.V.:\n- PR-2026-3802, submitted 18 June 2026, approved 20 June 2026 by Jonas Reiter (Level 2): \"Forelight ERP data connector \u2014 one-time licence and configuration so our SAP sales history can be loaded into the Forelight forecasting platform during the pilot.\" TCV EUR 14,000 (excl. VAT). Status: PO issued, delivered.\n- PR-2025-1190, approved 3 March 2025: \"Forelight webinar tickets \u2014 industry forecasting conference\", TCV EUR 450. Status: closed.\n\nProcureHub history \u2014 related suppliers:\n- ForeLogix Consulting GmbH (framework FA-11): PR-2026-3977, 2 July 2026, EUR 18,600 \u2014 \"S&OP process consulting\". ForeLogix is an unrelated company (different ownership; confirmed by the supplier master team).\n\nTreasury note: all amounts are in EUR; no conversion needed.\nInformation Security: review ticket SEC-8812 opened on 31 August 2026 (in progress).\nLegal: standard template, 12-month initial term; no Legal review required.\nBudget check: cost centre 4120 has EUR 60,000 remaining in the 2026 opex budget; 2027 budget to be confirmed in November.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.4876583218574524, "correct": false, "expected": "parts_covered_labour_charged", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-08", "item_sha256": "060ef587db2cf5980b9b525b8dbb91834c717082a94cb75528caad9e63f28c3d", "latency_ms": 435.53349655121565, "options": ["full_repair_no_charge", "parts_covered_labour_charged", "pro_rata_replacement_credit", "not_covered"], "prediction": "not_covered", "probabilities": {"full_repair_no_charge": 0.15831947326660156, "not_covered": 0.4876583218574524, "parts_covered_labour_charged": 0.2957797348499298, "pro_rata_replacement_credit": 0.05824248492717743}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "BREVANO ESPRESSO SYSTEMS \u2014 CONSUMER SERVICE CENTRE\nWARRANTY DETERMINATION PACKET, SERVICE ORDER SO-26-118374\n\nDOCUMENT 1 \u2014 LIMITED WARRANTY FOR HOME MACHINES, FORM WB-HOME-22 (APPLIES TO PURCHASES FROM 1 JANUARY 2022)\n\n1. Who gives this warranty\nThis warranty is given by Brevano Espresso Systems (\"Brevano\") to the first end-user purchaser of a new Brevano home espresso machine bought from Brevano or from an authorised retailer listed in Annex A. It is in addition to, and does not limit, your statutory rights against the seller.\n\n2. Definitions\n2.1 \"Purchase Date\" \u2014 the date shown on the original proof of purchase (receipt or invoice). If a machine was delivered later than it was paid for, the delivery date shown on the delivery note is used instead.\n2.2 \"Registration\" \u2014 completion of the online product registration at brevano-example.com/register (or by phone to the service centre) with the machine's serial number and Purchase Date. The registration date is the date on which the registration is recorded in Brevano's system.\n2.3 \"Commercial Use\" \u2014 use of the machine (a) in a caf\u00e9, bar, restaurant, hotel, shop or similar establishment, (b) in an office or workplace used by more than 10 people, or (c) to prepare drinks that are sold or otherwise supplied in return for payment. Use in a private household, including a home office used by the householder, is not Commercial Use.\n2.4 \"Parts\" \u2014 replacement components supplied by Brevano. \"Labour\" \u2014 the technician's time for diagnosis, disassembly, repair and testing, charged at the flat rates in Annex B.\n2.5 \"Wear Items\" \u2014 gaskets, shower screens, filter baskets, water filter cartridges, drip trays and cups.\n\n3. What is covered and for how long\n3.1 Standard coverage: for 24 months from the Purchase Date, Brevano will repair any defect in materials or workmanship free of charge for Parts and Labour.\n3.2 Extended parts coverage: if the machine was registered within 30 days of the Purchase Date, Brevano will additionally supply Parts free of charge for defects arising in months 25 to 36 after the Purchase Date. Labour in this period is charged to the customer at the flat rates in Annex B.\n3.3 Commercial Use: if the machine has been used for Commercial Use at any time, the coverage period in 3.1 is 12 months and 3.2 does not apply.\n3.4 Replacement: if Brevano determines that a covered defect cannot be repaired, or that the repair cost would exceed 70% of the current retail price of an equivalent machine, Brevano will instead provide a replacement credit. During the standard coverage period the credit is 100% of the original purchase price; during extended parts coverage it is reduced by 1/36 of the purchase price for each full month since the Purchase Date.\n3.5 Repairs and replacement parts do not extend the warranty period.\n\n4. What is not covered\n4.1 Wear Items (except when defective on delivery).\n4.2 Damage caused by limescale where the machine has not been descaled as instructed in the user manual. Where the machine's descaling log shows descaling at the recommended intervals, limescale is not grounds for refusal.\n4.3 Accidental damage, misuse, use of non-Brevano parts, repairs by unauthorised persons, use on incorrect voltage.\n4.4 Cosmetic damage not affecting function.\n4.5 Machines purchased from sellers not listed in Annex A (including grey imports and marketplace sellers), unless the machine was sold new with a Brevano-issued warranty card stamped by the seller.\n\n5. How to claim\n5.1 Contact the service centre with your proof of purchase and serial number. You will receive a service order and a prepaid shipping label or a technician appointment.\n5.2 Diagnosis is free if the claim is covered. If the claim is not covered, or if Labour is chargeable under 3.2, a quote will be sent before any work is carried out.\n\nANNEX A \u2014 AUTHORISED RETAILERS (EXTRACT, VALID 2024\u20132026)\nCasa Crema Stores; Kaffeehaus Direkt GmbH; R\u00f6sterei Nord & S\u00f6hne (online and shops); ElectroPlanet (all branches); Brevano Online Shop.\nNot authorised (known grey-market sellers, for staff information): Kaffeehaus Discount24; BestBuyCoffee Outlet; any marketplace seller not listed above.\n\nANNEX B \u2014 FLAT LABOUR RATES (FROM 1 JANUARY 2026)\nDiagnosis EUR 45; pump replacement EUR 85; boiler replacement EUR 140; brew group service EUR 60; electronics board replacement EUR 70.\n\nDOCUMENT 2 \u2014 SERVICE BULLETIN SB-2024-07 (ISSUED 25 SEPTEMBER 2024)\nSubject: Registration portal outage and extended registration window\nBetween 1 August and 20 September 2024 the product registration portal was intermittently unavailable and many phone registrations could not be completed. To ensure customers are not disadvantaged, for machines with a Purchase Date between 1 July 2024 and 30 September 2024 inclusive, a registration recorded within 60 days of the Purchase Date is treated as a registration within 30 days for the purposes of clause 3.2 of WB-HOME-22. All other terms remain unchanged. This bulletin does not change the length of standard coverage.\n\nDOCUMENT 3 \u2014 SERVICE BULLETIN SB-2025-02 (ISSUED 3 MARCH 2025)\nSubject: Pump supplier change\nFrom serial number range BH-6-25xxxxx onward, the vibratory pump is sourced from a new supplier. Pumps from the previous supplier remain fully supported; replacement pumps for older machines are the new part number P-4410-B. No change to warranty terms.\n\nDOCUMENT 3A \u2014 SERVICE CENTRE OPERATING NOTES (INTERNAL)\n- Shipping: prepaid labels are issued for all warranty claims, including claims where Labour will be charged. Machines must be emptied of water and beans and shipped in the original box or the loan box supplied by Brevano.\n- Loan machines: offered only during standard coverage (months 1\u201324) or where the repair takes longer than 10 business days.\n- Quotes: any chargeable amount must be quoted to the customer in writing and accepted before work starts. Unaccepted quotes expire after 14 days, and the machine is returned unrepaired at no charge for return shipping.\n- Goodwill: team leaders may approve goodwill of up to EUR 50 per case for customer-experience reasons. Goodwill is recorded separately and does not change the warranty determination recorded in the system.\n- Recording: the warranty determination field has four values \u2014 \"Full warranty\", \"Parts only\", \"Replacement credit\", \"Not covered\".\n\nDOCUMENT 4 \u2014 CUSTOMER AND MACHINE RECORD\nCustomer: Ms Renate Voskuijlen, home address in Utrecht region.\nMachine: Brevano Aria Duo, serial BH-6-24118093.\nProof of purchase: invoice no. KD-2024-778120 from Kaffeehaus Direkt GmbH, dated 10 August 2024, price EUR 749.00, paid by card. Delivery note: delivered 10 August 2024 (collected in shop).\nRegistration: recorded in the Brevano system on 22 September 2024 (via phone). Customer note on the registration: \"Tried online in August several times, the site kept failing.\"\nCustomer profile: the registration form lists the address both as home address and as the business address of \"RV Vertalingen\" (sole-trader translation business run from the customer's home study; no employees).\nPrevious service: none.\n\nDOCUMENT 5 \u2014 SERVICE ORDER SO-26-118374\nOpened: 2 September 2026, by phone.\nCustomer description: \"Machine powers on, lights normal, but no water comes through the group. Pump hums very loudly for a second and then stops. Started last week.\"\nMachine received at service centre: 9 September 2026.\nTechnician report (T. Haverkamp, 11 September 2026):\n- Descaling log (read from the machine's memory): descaled every 6\u20138 weeks as recommended; last descaling 14 August 2026.\n- Light scale in the brew group; not causal.\n- Pump motor winding open circuit; pump seized. Defect in materials (winding insulation breakdown). Not caused by scale, misuse or voltage.\n- Repair: replace pump with P-4410-B. Parts retail price EUR 62.00. Labour: pump replacement.\n- Other findings: group gasket worn (wear item) \u2014 recommended replacement at customer's cost EUR 9.50; customer declined.\n- Repair is technically possible; estimated total repair cost well below 70% of the current retail price of an equivalent machine (EUR 799).\nFront-desk note (J. Olde, 11 September 2026): \"Purchased Aug 2024, so it's over 24 months. Registration was 43 days after purchase \u2014 outside the 30 days. Looks like no coverage; also the customer has a business registered at that address. Suggest sending a full paid-repair quote.\"\nCustomer email (12 September 2026): \"I use the machine only at home \u2014 two coffees a day, I don't sell anything. My translation business is just me at my desk. Please let me know what the repair will cost.\"\nParts availability: P-4410-B in stock (warehouse Venlo, 140 units). Estimated turnaround after quote acceptance: 3 business days, then return shipment.\nLoan machine: not requested by the customer.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.5172608494758606, "correct": true, "expected": "license_required", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-09", "item_sha256": "c84c82f9c12a09284844f9735b66084baeb7a8fa849faed2518fd4ad6b08aeb9", "latency_ms": 458.5908865556121, "options": ["no_license_required", "license_exception_lvs", "license_required", "shipment_prohibited"], "prediction": "license_required", "probabilities": {"license_exception_lvs": 0.24433673918247223, "license_required": 0.5172608494758606, "no_license_required": 0.19028964638710022, "shipment_prohibited": 0.04811275377869606}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "VELANT OPTICS GROUP \u2014 TRADE COMPLIANCE OFFICE\nEXPORT DETERMINATION WORKSHEET, ORDER SO-MR-26-0931 (fictional regulatory regime used for internal training and live screening)\n\nPART 1 \u2014 NORTHERN UNION STRATEGIC GOODS REGULATION (SGR) \u2014 CONSOLIDATED EXTRACT FOR STAFF USE\n\nArticle 1 \u2014 Scope\n1.1 The SGR controls the export and re-export of dual-use items listed in Annex I (\"controlled items\"), and of other items where Article 6 (restricted parties) or Article 8 (embargoes) applies.\n1.2 An item is \"subject to the SGR\" if (a) it is located in the Northern Union (NU), (b) it was produced in the NU, wherever it is now located, or (c) it was produced outside the NU and incorporates NU-origin controlled content above the de minimis level in Article 4.\n1.3 Items not subject to the SGR are outside its controls, except where another Article expressly provides otherwise.\n\nArticle 2 \u2014 Definitions\n2.1 \"Party to the transaction\" \u2014 the purchaser, the intermediate consignee(s), the ultimate consignee and the end user.\n2.2 \"NU-origin controlled content\" \u2014 components, software or technology of NU origin that are themselves listed in Annex I, valued at their purchase price when incorporated.\n2.3 \"De minimis percentage\" \u2014 the value of NU-origin controlled content divided by the ex-works selling price of the foreign-produced item, expressed as a percentage.\n2.4 \"Restricted Parties List\" (RPL) \u2014 the list published in Annex IV, including every name, alias (\"also known as\") and address shown in an entry. A party matches an entry if its name corresponds to the entry name or to any listed alias, or if it is located at a listed address and is under common control with the listed entity.\n2.5 \"Shipment value\" \u2014 the invoice value of all items shipped at one time to one ultimate consignee.\n\nArticle 3 \u2014 Country groups (extract)\nGroup A: NU member states. Group B: close partner countries (Aldmere, Kestovia, Ruhland, Tessaly). Group C: other cooperating countries (Corvania, Marisco, Oronta, Pellan, Vasque). Group D: countries of concern for diversion (Draskel, Ilvaria). Group E: embargoed destinations (Karth, Soltane).\n\nArticle 4 \u2014 De minimis\n4.1 A foreign-produced item is not subject to the SGR under 1.2(c) if its de minimis percentage does not exceed: 25% for destinations in Groups B and C; 10% for destinations in Group D.\n4.2 There is no de minimis level for destinations in Group E, or for items incorporating NU-origin content classified 3E-901 or 5E-202 (all such items are subject to the SGR).\n\nArticle 5 \u2014 Licence requirements for controlled items (country chart, extract)\nCategory 3A-610 (thermal imaging components and modules): licence required for Groups C, D and E; not required for Groups A and B.\nCategory 5A-202 (encryption hardware): licence required for Groups D and E only.\nCategory 6A-003 (cameras, general): licence required for Group E only.\n\nArticle 6 \u2014 Restricted parties\n6.1 A licence is required to export or re-export any item subject to the SGR where any party to the transaction matches an entry on the RPL. Licence applications under 6.1 are reviewed with a presumption of denial.\n6.2 [Inserted by Amendment 2026/14 \u2014 see Part 2.]\n\nArticle 7 \u2014 Licence exceptions\n7.1 LVS (low value shipments): a licence required under Article 5 is not needed for shipments of Category 3A-610 or 6A-003 items to Group B or Group C destinations where the shipment value does not exceed EUR 5,000 and no more than two LVS shipments are made to the same ultimate consignee in a calendar year.\n7.2 TMP (temporary exports for demonstration, returned within 12 months) \u2014 Groups A\u2013C.\n7.3 General restriction: no licence exception may be used where any party to the transaction matches an RPL entry, or where the destination is in Group E.\n\nArticle 8 \u2014 Embargoes\n8.1 Exports and re-exports of any item, whether or not subject to the SGR, to Group E destinations or to persons located there are prohibited.\n\nArticle 9 \u2014 Records\nExporters keep classification, screening and licence records for five years.\n\nArticle 10 \u2014 Voluntary disclosure\n10.1 An exporter that discovers a possible breach should notify the competent authority in writing within 30 days of discovery, with a narrative account, the items and parties involved, and remedial steps taken.\n10.2 Voluntary disclosure is a mitigating factor in any penalty determination.\n\nArticle 11 \u2014 Penalties\n11.1 Exporting without a required licence, or on the basis of an unavailable licence exception, is an offence punishable by a fine of up to EUR 500,000 or five times the value of the goods, whichever is higher, for legal persons.\n11.2 Knowingly supplying items to a party on the RPL is an aggravated offence.\n\nArticle 12 \u2014 Transitional provisions\n12.1 Licences granted before an amendment enters into force remain valid for their stated term.\n12.2 A shipment is assessed under the rules in force on the date the items leave the exporter's control, not on the date of the order or the invoice.\n\nPART 2 \u2014 AMENDMENT 2026/14 (PUBLISHED 12 MAY 2026, IN FORCE 1 JUNE 2026)\n(1) New Article 6.2: \"Article 6.1 also applies to any foreign-produced item that incorporates NU-origin controlled content, irrespective of the de minimis percentage. For the purposes of Article 6, such an item is treated as subject to the SGR.\"\n(2) Annex I Category 3A-610: technical parameter (b) is revised from 640\u00d7480 to 1024\u00d7768 pixels. [Not relevant to FPGA controller sub-item 3A-610.c.]\n(3) Annex IV (RPL): entries 41\u201357 added (see Part 3).\n\nPART 3 \u2014 ANNEX IV RESTRICTED PARTIES LIST (EXTRACT)\nEntry 12: Ostvik Marine Supply \u2014 Port Road 4, Pellan City, Pellan.\nEntry 38: Draskel Photonics Institute \u2014 also known as: DPI; Institute 17 \u2014 Draskel.\nEntry 44 (added 1 June 2026): HPC Holdings Ltd \u2014 also known as: Harrow Precision Components; Harrow PC Ltd; HPC Optronics \u2014 Unit 9, Delmar Industrial Park, Corvania; 118 Canal Street, Oronta Bay, Oronta.\nEntry 51 (added 1 June 2026): Silvan Trade FZE \u2014 also known as: STF Logistics \u2014 Free Zone 3, Vasque.\n\nPART 4 \u2014 ORDER SO-MR-26-0931\nSeller / exporter: Velant Optics Pte Ltd (Marisco) \u2014 a Velant Group subsidiary; production site in Marisco.\nItem: TX-40 thermal camera core module, 384\u00d7288 resolution, uncooled microbolometer; ex-works price EUR 1,400 per unit.\nQuantity: 3 units. Invoice value: EUR 4,200. Incoterms: FCA Marisco airport.\nClassification of the TX-40 (Velant Classification Record VCR-TX40-v3): produced in Marisco (outside NU); if subject to the SGR, it would be classified 3A-610.\nBill of materials (controlled content): FPGA controller type ZE-7 of NU origin (Annex I category 3A-610.c), purchase price EUR 252 per unit. No other NU-origin controlled content. No 3E-901 or 5E-202 content.\nPurchaser and ultimate consignee: Harrow Precision Components, Unit 9, Delmar Industrial Park, Corvania.\nEnd user (declared on end-use statement): same; end use \"integration into industrial furnace monitoring cameras\".\nIntermediate consignee: none (direct air freight).\nPrevious shipments to this consignee in 2026: none. Previous LVS shipments in 2026 to any consignee in Corvania: one, to a different company.\nRequested ship date: 24 September 2026.\n\nPART 5 \u2014 SCREENING AND ANALYST NOTES\nAutomated screening (ScreenPro v8, run 15 September 2026, exact-name mode): \"Harrow Precision Components\" \u2014 result: NO EXACT MATCH ON ENTRY NAME. Fuzzy mode not run.\nAnalyst note (junior analyst, 15 September 2026):\n\"De minimis: 252 / 1,400 = 18%, below the 25% level for Group C, so the TX-40 is not subject to the SGR and no licence is needed. Even if it were subject, it's under EUR 5,000, so LVS would cover it. Screening clean. Recommend NLR (no licence required) and release.\"\nSales note (15 September 2026): \"Customer is urgent \u2014 they need the modules for a furnace line commissioning on 1 October. Please release this week.\"\nLogistics note: \"Air freight booked on 24 Sept; can move to 26 Sept at no cost.\"\n\nPART 6 \u2014 VELANT GROUP INTERNAL COMPLIANCE PROGRAMME (ICP) EXTRACT\nICP-3.1 Every order for a classified item, and every order to a new customer, must be screened against the RPL in both exact-name and fuzzy (alias and address) mode before release. Exact-name screening alone is not sufficient.\nICP-3.2 Determinations are recorded in the worksheet using one of four outcomes: NLR, licence exception (state which), licence required, or prohibited.\nICP-3.3 A licence required determination blocks the order in the ERP system until a licence number is entered.\nICP-4.1 Sales staff may not contact the authority or the customer about licensing without the Trade Compliance Office.\nICP-5.2 The Trade Compliance Office reviews any determination made by a junior analyst before release.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.6513549089431763, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-11", "item_sha256": "06e29a5c4a2be50776f58fc61b708f270307a296c81f4fd3f42f0baacf6ab04d", "latency_ms": 419.4854227825999, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.3486451506614685, "yes": 0.6513549089431763}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "MERIDIAN CARD SERVICES \u2014 DISPUTES OPERATIONS\nCASE REVIEW PACKET: DISPUTE DP-26-0703318 (PAXNET CREDIT CARD)\n\n=== EXCERPT 1: PAXNET DISPUTE RULES FOR ISSUERS (EDITION APRIL 2026), CHAPTER 9 ===\n\n9.0 General\n9.0.1 The issuer may raise a dispute on behalf of a cardholder only under a valid reason code and within the time limits of that reason code. Rules in this chapter are to be applied by the issuer's dispute analyst at each stage (initial dispute, pre-arbitration). The analyst's decision at the representment review stage is whether the dispute is decided in the cardholder's favour (the provisional credit is made final and the merchant is debited) or in the merchant's favour (the provisional credit is reversed).\n9.0.2 Days are calendar days unless stated otherwise.\n9.0.3 A merchant's compelling evidence must be assessed against the specific requirements of the reason code; general statements (\"the item was shipped\", \"the customer is a repeat buyer\") are not compelling evidence.\n\n9.1 Reason code 13.1 \u2014 Merchandise or services not received\n9.1.1 Use: the cardholder paid for merchandise or services that were not received by the agreed date or location.\n9.1.2 Pre-conditions: (a) the cardholder attempted to resolve the dispute with the merchant, and either the merchant refused, did not respond within 7 days, or the merchant is no longer trading; (b) the cardholder has not received the merchandise at the time of the dispute.\n9.1.3 Time limit: the dispute must be raised within 120 days of the expected delivery date where the purchase terms stated an expected delivery date or a latest delivery date; otherwise within 120 days of the transaction processing date. In any case not later than 540 days after the transaction processing date.\n9.1.4 Merchant compelling evidence (representment) is limited to:\n   (a) proof of delivery to the address given by the cardholder at the time of purchase, such as carrier tracking showing delivery to that address;\n   (b) proof of delivery to a collection point, parcel locker or other pickup location, but only where (i) the cardholder selected that pickup location at checkout or later authorised the change in writing, or (ii) the merchant also provides evidence that the cardholder or a person authorised by the cardholder collected the item (for example a locker log showing collection with the cardholder's PIN, or a signed pickup receipt);\n   (c) evidence that the cardholder has used the merchandise or services (for example online account activity for digital goods);\n   (d) evidence that the cardholder agreed to a new delivery date that has not yet passed.\n9.1.5 A carrier-initiated redirection of a parcel (for example after a failed delivery attempt) does not by itself constitute the cardholder's selection or authorisation of the pickup location, even if the carrier notified the cardholder by email or text message.\n9.1.6 A merchant offer to re-ship or refund made after the dispute has been raised does not invalidate the dispute unless the cardholder accepts it.\n\n9.2 Reason code 13.3 \u2014 Not as described or defective merchandise\n9.2.1 The cardholder must have returned or attempted to return the merchandise, unless the merchant refused to provide return instructions.\n9.2.2 Time limit: 120 days from the date of receipt of the merchandise.\n\n9.3 Reason code 13.6 \u2014 Credit not processed\n9.3.1 Use where the merchant provided a credit voucher or a refund acknowledgement that was not processed. Time limit: 120 days from the date on the credit acknowledgement; if undated, 120 days from the date of the cardholder's return of the goods.\n\n9.4 Reason code 10.4 \u2014 Card-absent fraud\n9.4.1 The cardholder must certify that they did not authorise or participate in the transaction. Transactions authenticated with strong customer authentication are generally not eligible.\n\n=== EXCERPT 2: PAXNET MEMBER BULLETIN 2026-11 (EFFECTIVE 1 JUNE 2026) ===\n(1) For reason code 13.1, signature on delivery is no longer required as part of proof of delivery under 9.1.4(a) for transactions below EUR 250. For transactions of EUR 250 or more, carrier tracking with GPS or photo evidence of delivery at the address is sufficient.\n(2) Time limits in 9.1.3 are unchanged.\n(3) Reason code 13.3 now allows photographic evidence of defects in place of an independent expert opinion for goods under EUR 1,000.\n\n=== EXCERPT 3: MERIDIAN INTERNAL PROCEDURE DSP-04 (DISPUTE ANALYST DESK GUIDE) ===\n- Provisional credit is granted to the cardholder when a dispute is raised and remains in place until the representment review.\n- The analyst records the outcome as \"Cardholder favour\" or \"Merchant favour\" with the rule reference.\n- Fees: Paxnet charges the issuer EUR 15 per pre-arbitration filing; this does not affect the analyst's decision.\n- Where the analyst cannot decide on the evidence, request more information from the cardholder with a 10-day deadline (not needed where the rules decide the case).\n- Communications: the cardholder is informed of the outcome by secure message within 2 business days of the decision, with a plain-language explanation. Do not quote Paxnet rule numbers to the cardholder.\n- Repeat disputers: cardholders with more than three disputes in 12 months are referred to the Card Risk team after the case is decided; this referral never changes the outcome of an individual dispute.\n- Merchant contact: analysts must not contact the merchant directly; all communication goes through the Paxnet dispute platform.\n- Service level: representment reviews are completed within 5 business days of receipt of the representment.\n\n=== CASE FILE DP-26-0703318 ===\nCardholder: Mr Oskar Lindqvist-Barre, card ending 4471 (Meridian Platinum).\nMerchant: Soundhaven Audio Online (merchant category 5732, electronics), merchant ID SH-99210.\nTransaction: 2 March 2026, EUR 389.00, \"AuraSound X9 wireless headphones \u2014 PRE-ORDER\". Transaction processing date: 2 March 2026. 3-D Secure authenticated.\nOrder confirmation (supplied by cardholder): order SH-2026-118830; \"Pre-order: ships no later than 20 March 2026; expected delivery by 20 March 2026.\" Delivery method selected at checkout: \"Standard home delivery\" to Flat 3, 14 Harbour Mews, Lindhaven. Pickup/locker options were available at checkout but not selected.\n\nCardholder statement (dispute form, 11 July 2026):\n\"I never received the headphones. In late March I got a text from the carrier saying a delivery attempt failed and the parcel was at a ParcelPoint locker, but I was abroad on business until 9 April and by then the tracking said 'returned to sender'. I assumed Soundhaven would refund me. I emailed them on 28 June 2026 asking for my money back or the headphones and got no reply. I did not authorise any redirection.\"\nCardholder's email to merchant of 28 June 2026: attached (sent to support@soundhaven-example.com; no reply recorded).\nDispute raised by Meridian: 11 July 2026, reason code 13.1. Provisional credit EUR 389.00 posted 11 July 2026.\n\nMerchant representment (received 29 July 2026):\n\"The order was shipped on 18 March 2026 with tracking number PP-7730-44812. The carrier attempted delivery on 23 March and, as the customer was not home, redirected the parcel to ParcelPoint locker #4471 (Station Square, Lindhaven) on 24 March. The carrier notified the customer by text message with the collection PIN. Delivery was therefore completed. In addition the dispute is late \u2014 it was raised 131 days after the transaction. Finally, on 15 July 2026 we offered to re-ship the headphones free of charge, which the customer ignored. We request reversal of the dispute.\"\nAttachments: carrier tracking history; screenshot of carrier SMS template; email of 15 July 2026 offering reshipment.\n\nCarrier tracking history (from representment):\n18 Mar 2026 16:02 \u2014 Shipment received by carrier\n23 Mar 2026 11:47 \u2014 Delivery attempted, recipient not available (Harbour Mews)\n24 Mar 2026 08:15 \u2014 Redirected by carrier to ParcelPoint locker #4471, Station Square\n24 Mar 2026 09:30 \u2014 Delivered to locker; collection PIN sent to recipient\n31 Mar 2026 18:00 \u2014 Not collected within 7-day storage period; returning to sender\n04 Apr 2026 10:12 \u2014 Returned to sender (Soundhaven warehouse)\n\nCardholder reply to the 15 July offer (forwarded to Meridian 20 July 2026): \"No thank you \u2014 I bought a different pair months ago. I want the refund.\"\n\nPrevious disputes by this cardholder: one in 2023 (reason 10.4, card-absent fraud, decided in cardholder favour). No disputes with this merchant before.\nAnalyst assignment: representment review opened 30 July 2026.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.851952850818634, "correct": false, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-13", "item_sha256": "c1d8bbd1af8f4668b3ca011d749dddcea14a2fd7b677c0552a1feee1b05a2154", "latency_ms": 420.03131937235594, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.851952850818634, "yes": 0.14804719388484955}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "WAYFARER ASSURANCE LTD \u2014 SINGLE TRIP TRAVEL INSURANCE\nPOLICY WORDING WT-2025 (EXTRACT), ENDORSEMENT WT-E7, AND CLAIM FILE TC-2026-58114\n\nPART 1 \u2014 POLICY WORDING WT-2025 (EXTRACT)\n\nSECTION 1 \u2014 DEFINITIONS\nIn this policy, words in capitals have the meanings below.\n1.1 \"You/Your/Insured Person\" \u2014 each person named on the Certificate of Insurance.\n1.2 \"Trip\" \u2014 the journey described on the Certificate, including the departure and return dates.\n1.3 \"Family Member\" \u2014 Your spouse or domestic partner; Your or Your spouse's parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, step-child, grandchild, brother, sister, legal guardian or ward. Friends and travel companions who are not Family Members are not Family Members even if they live with You.\n1.4 \"Coverage Effective Date\" \u2014 for Trip Cancellation cover, 12:01 a.m. local time on the day after the premium is paid; for all other cover, the Trip departure date.\n1.5 \"Look-Back Period\" \u2014 the 60 days immediately before and including the Coverage Effective Date.\n1.6 \"Pre-existing Condition\" \u2014 an illness, disease or other condition of You or of a Family Member for which, during the Look-Back Period, the person: (a) was hospitalised; (b) received, or was recommended by a doctor to receive, new treatment or a change in treatment; (c) had a Change in Medication; or (d) experienced symptoms that worsened or were new and were not yet diagnosed.\n1.7 \"Change in Medication\" \u2014 a new prescription, an increase or decrease in dosage, or the discontinuation of a prescribed medicine. The following are not a Change in Medication: switching between a brand-name medicine and a generic equivalent at the same dosage; routine repeat prescriptions (refills) at the same dosage; a change in the manufacturer of the same generic medicine.\n1.8 \"Treatment\" \u2014 medical, surgical or other care provided by a doctor, including prescribing medicine. A routine check-up or monitoring visit at which the doctor makes no change to the person's care is not Treatment.\n1.9 \"Initial Trip Deposit\" \u2014 the first payment made towards the cost of the Trip.\n1.10 \"Stable\" \u2014 this term is not used in this policy and has no bearing on cover; cover depends only on the definitions above.\n\nSECTION 2 \u2014 TRIP CANCELLATION\n2.1 We will reimburse the non-refundable, prepaid Trip costs You forfeit if You cancel the Trip before the departure date because of one of the covered reasons below, occurring after the Coverage Effective Date:\n   (a) unforeseen illness, injury or death of You, a Family Member or a travel companion named on the Certificate, which is severe enough to make it medically necessary to cancel the Trip or, for a Family Member, requires Your presence;\n   (b) jury duty or a court subpoena You could not postpone;\n   (c) Your home being made uninhabitable by fire, flood or burglary within 10 days before departure;\n   (d) a mandatory evacuation order at the destination.\n2.2 Maximum benefit: the Trip cost shown on the Certificate, up to GBP 10,000 per Insured Person.\n2.3 You must cancel with the travel supplier(s) within 72 hours of the covered reason arising, and notify us within 30 days.\n\nSECTION 3 \u2014 TRIP INTERRUPTION AND DELAY (summary)\n3.1 Interruption: unused prepaid costs and additional transport home, for the same covered reasons as Section 2, occurring after departure.\n3.2 Delay: GBP 150 per full 12 hours of delay of the outbound common-carrier departure, maximum GBP 600.\n\nSECTION 4 \u2014 MEDICAL EXPENSES ABROAD (summary)\nUp to GBP 5,000,000 for emergency medical treatment and repatriation; excess GBP 100 per claim.\n\nSECTION 5 \u2014 GENERAL EXCLUSIONS\n5.1 Pre-existing Conditions. We will not pay any claim under any section arising from a Pre-existing Condition, unless the waiver in 5.2 applies.\n5.2 Pre-existing Condition waiver. Exclusion 5.1 does not apply if (a) the premium was paid within 14 days of the Initial Trip Deposit; (b) the full non-refundable Trip cost was insured; and (c) You were medically able to travel when the premium was paid.\n5.3 Travel against medical advice, or for the purpose of obtaining medical treatment.\n5.4 Pregnancy, except unforeseen complications before week 28.\n5.5 Hazardous activities listed in Appendix B, unless the Adventure Pack is purchased.\n5.6 Travel to a country where the government has advised against all travel at the time of booking.\n5.7 Losses due to the financial default of a travel supplier, unless the Supplier Default Pack is purchased.\n\nSECTION 6 \u2014 CLAIMS CONDITIONS\n6.1 You must provide the Certificate, receipts for the Trip costs, the supplier's cancellation invoice showing amounts refunded and forfeited, and a medical certificate from the treating doctor.\n6.2 Where a claim relates to the condition of a Family Member, we may request that person's medical records for the 12 months before the Coverage Effective Date. We will consider these records only to apply the policy definitions.\n\nSECTION 7 \u2014 COMPLAINTS AND GOVERNING LAW\n7.1 If You are unhappy with our service, contact Customer Relations; we will acknowledge within 5 business days and respond fully within 8 weeks. You may then refer the complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Service.\n7.2 This policy is governed by the law of England and Wales.\n\nAPPENDIX B \u2014 HAZARDOUS ACTIVITIES (EXTRACT)\nCovered without the Adventure Pack: hiking below 2,500 m on marked trails; snorkelling; cycling on roads and marked tracks; guided city tours; wine tasting tours; kayaking on inland waters with a guide.\nCovered only with the Adventure Pack: scuba diving below 18 m; skiing and snowboarding off-piste; mountaineering with ropes; motorcycling above 125 cc; paragliding; white-water rafting above grade 3.\nNever covered: professional sports; motor racing; base jumping; expeditions to polar regions.\n\nAPPENDIX C \u2014 HOW WE PAY\nClaims are paid by bank transfer to the Insured Person within 10 business days of acceptance. Amounts in foreign currency are converted at the rate on the date the cost was incurred. Where a travel supplier later refunds any amount, You must repay that amount to us.\n\nPART 2 \u2014 ENDORSEMENT WT-E7 (APPLIES TO POLICIES PURCHASED ON OR AFTER 1 MARCH 2026)\n(1) In 5.2(a), \"14 days\" is replaced by \"21 days\".\n(2) Trip Delay benefit in 3.2 increased to GBP 200 per 12 hours, maximum GBP 800.\n(3) No other changes.\n\nPART 3 \u2014 CLAIM FILE TC-2026-58114\n\nInsured Person: Ms Harriet Onwuachi.\nCertificate: WT-2026-3304417, premium paid online 27 April 2026, 19:42 (policy purchased on that date). Trip cost insured: GBP 3,860 (full non-refundable cost).\nTrip: two-week guided tour, Lisbon and Porto, departure 20 June 2026, return 4 July 2026.\nInitial Trip Deposit: GBP 500 paid to Solace Tours on 2 April 2026; balance paid 20 May 2026.\n\nCovered reason claimed: illness of Family Member \u2014 Ms Onwuachi's mother, Mrs Grace Onwuachi (age 81), admitted to hospital on 14 June 2026 with acute decompensated heart failure; discharged 29 June 2026. Treating consultant certifies that the daughter's presence was required.\nCancellation: Ms Onwuachi cancelled with Solace Tours on 15 June 2026 at 09:10. Forfeited: GBP 3,860 (no refund under the tour operator's terms within 30 days of departure). Claim notified to Wayfarer 18 June 2026.\n\nMother's medical records (GP and cardiology, requested under 6.2), relevant entries:\n- 2019: diagnosis of chronic heart failure; on daily diuretic \"Diurel\" and a beta-blocker since then.\n- 17 February 2026: GP review for ankle swelling; Diurel increased from 40 mg to 80 mg daily. Beta-blocker unchanged.\n- 25 February 2026: GP telephone follow-up: \"swelling resolved; continue Diurel 80 mg\".\n- 29 March 2026: pharmacy switched Diurel (brand) to generic furosemide-equivalent \"Diuretab\" 80 mg daily at the same dose; prescription reissued as generic.\n- 10 April 2026: routine cardiology clinic (annual): \"Clinically well, euvolaemic, NYHA class II. No change to medication. Review in 12 months.\"\n- 24 April 2026: repeat prescription Diuretab 80 mg daily (refill), beta-blocker (refill).\n- 14 June 2026: emergency admission, acute decompensated heart failure following a chest infection.\n\nClaims handler worksheet (first review, 2 July 2026):\n\"Waiver: deposit 2 April, premium 27 April = 25 days. Even with WT-E7 (21 days) this is outside the waiver window, so the waiver doesn't apply. Mother has long-standing heart failure with a dose increase this year and a change of medicine in March, and was seen by cardiology in April. Condition is clearly pre-existing -> decline under 5.1.\"\n\nTeam leader comment (3 July 2026): \"Please check the look-back dates carefully against the definitions before we send the decline letter.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.7328982949256897, "correct": true, "expected": "database_oncall", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-17", "item_sha256": "f2bf617097271932de50351b27779aba3b5ff1965a1a863de449c3573ba8252c", "latency_ms": 418.43664553016424, "options": ["security_incident_response", "no_page_ticket_only", "database_oncall", "vendor_support", "platform_oncall"], "prediction": "database_oncall", "probabilities": {"database_oncall": 0.7328982949256897, "no_page_ticket_only": 0.11239359527826309, "platform_oncall": 0.11239359527826309, "security_incident_response": 0.02507839910686016, "vendor_support": 0.017236115410923958}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "PELAGOS STREAMING \u2014 OPERATIONS\nALERT ROUTING RUNBOOK RB-ALERT-7 (REVISION 5, 2 AUGUST 2026), COMPONENT INVENTORY EXTRACT, CHANGE CALENDAR EXTRACT, AND ALERT STREAM FOR THE NIGHT OF 17\u201318 SEPTEMBER 2026\n\n=== RB-ALERT-7 REVISION 5 ===\n\n1. Purpose\nThis runbook tells the alert router (a human \"router of the night\" plus the PagerRelay rules engine that implements this runbook) where every production alert must go. It exists because in 2025 we paged the wrong team four times during high-severity events and lost an average of 23 minutes each time.\n\n2. Destinations\n- security_incident_response \u2014 the Security Incident Response Team (SIRT) on-call.\n- database_oncall \u2014 the Data Platform on-call (owns self-managed databases: PostgreSQL clusters, Redis, and their replicas).\n- platform_oncall \u2014 the Core Platform on-call (Kubernetes, networking, service mesh, application runtime, anything not owned by another destination).\n- vendor_support \u2014 open a priority case with the vendor that manages the component, and create a ticket for the platform team (no page).\n- no_page_ticket_only \u2014 no page; a ticket is created for the owning team's next business day.\n\n3. Routing rules \u2014 apply in this order; the first rule that matches decides the destination\nRule 1 \u2014 Security signals. Alerts from the IDS (severity high or critical), the cloud audit anomaly detector, and the secrets scanner go to security_incident_response.\n   Exception 1a: secrets-scanner findings whose fingerprint appears on the Known Test Fixtures allowlist (KTF list) are routed to no_page_ticket_only.\nRule 2 \u2014 Maintenance suppression. An alert is routed to no_page_ticket_only if ALL of the following hold: (a) the alert fires during an active maintenance window recorded in the change calendar for the component that raised it; (b) the alert type appears on that window's \"expected alerts\" list. A maintenance window is active only between its start and end times and only while its status in the change calendar is \"Scheduled\" or \"In progress\". A window with status \"Cancelled\" or \"Postponed\" is not active at any time.\nRule 3 \u2014 Database alerts. For self-managed database components: replica lag above 300 seconds sustained for at least 5 minutes; primary failover; disk usage above 90%; connection saturation above 95% for 10 minutes \u2192 database_oncall.\nRule 4 \u2014 Vendor-managed components. Any alert from a component marked \"vendor-managed\" in the component inventory \u2192 vendor_support.\nRule 5 \u2014 Default. Anything else \u2192 platform_oncall.\n\n4. Definitions\n4.1 \"Component\" \u2014 an entry in the component inventory (Section B below). Alerts carry the component ID in the field comp_id.\n4.2 \"Self-managed\" \u2014 deployed and operated by Pelagos staff on Pelagos infrastructure. \"Vendor-managed\" \u2014 operated by a third party under a managed-service contract, even if the data belongs to Pelagos.\n4.3 \"Sustained\" \u2014 the condition held at every evaluation in the stated period (evaluations every 30 seconds).\n\n5. Router responsibilities\n5.1 The router confirms PagerRelay's decision within 5 minutes. If PagerRelay's configuration is stale (for example a maintenance window was cancelled after PagerRelay loaded it), the router overrides PagerRelay and applies this runbook.\n5.2 The router never pages more than one destination for a single alert; correlated alerts are linked in the incident tool.\n5.3 Escalation: if a page is not acknowledged within 10 minutes, it escalates to the secondary of the same destination, then to the duty manager.\n\n6. Changes in revision 5\n6.1 Rule 2 now requires the window's status to be Scheduled or In progress (previously any window in the calendar suppressed alerts).\n6.2 Redis moved from platform ownership to Data Platform (Rule 3).\n6.3 Rule 4 clarified: vendor_support is used even when the vendor-managed component is a database.\n\n7. Hand-over and records\n7.1 At 08:00 and 20:00 UTC the router hands over to the next router with a list of open alerts, suppressed alerts and any overrides made under 5.1.\n7.2 Every override of PagerRelay is recorded in the incident tool with the rule applied and a one-line reason. Overrides are reviewed weekly by the Reliability team to improve PagerRelay configuration.\n7.3 Suppressed alerts remain visible in the alert console with the label \"suppressed\" and the window ID.\n\n8. Paging etiquette\n8.1 Pages include: alert ID, component, one-line summary, dashboard link and the rule applied.\n8.2 Do not page a destination \"for information\". Use the #ops-fyi channel instead.\n8.3 Where an alert could plausibly belong to two destinations, apply the rules in order; do not page both.\n\n9. Known false-positive sources (informational)\n- checkout-svc pod restarts during node pool rotation (Wednesdays 00:00\u201302:00 UTC).\n- CDN origin 5xx below 1% during Fastwave certificate updates.\n- Secrets-scanner findings in tests/fixtures directories; most are on the KTF list.\n- Analytics replica lag warnings during the nightly reporting batch (00:30\u201302:30 UTC).\n\n=== COMPONENT INVENTORY (EXTRACT) ===\ncomp_id | name | type | management | owner\nC-1002 | orders-db-primary | PostgreSQL 15 primary | self-managed | Data Platform\nC-1003 | orders-db-replica-1 | PostgreSQL streaming replica | self-managed | Data Platform\nC-1004 | orders-db-replica-2 | PostgreSQL streaming replica | self-managed | Data Platform\nC-1010 | orders-db-ro-analytics | managed read replica (DataHarbor Cloud) | vendor-managed | Data Platform (contract owner)\nC-2201 | edge-cdn | CDN (Fastwave) | vendor-managed | Platform\nC-3050 | events-kafka | Kafka cluster (StreamOps managed) | vendor-managed | Platform\nC-4100 | checkout-svc | Kubernetes deployment | self-managed | Payments squad\nC-9001 | repo-scanner | secrets scanner on source repositories | self-managed | Security Engineering\n\n=== CHANGE CALENDAR (EXTRACT) ===\nMW-2231 | Component: C-1002, C-1003, C-1004 (orders-db cluster) | Window: 18 Sep 2026 01:00\u201303:00 UTC | Purpose: PostgreSQL minor version upgrade 15.7 \u2192 15.8, rolling | Expected alerts: replica lag, primary failover, connection saturation | Status history: created 10 Sep (Scheduled); 17 Sep 23:52 UTC changed to \"Postponed\" by R. Castellanos \u2014 note: \"vendor advisory: 15.8 has a logical replication regression, postponing to next week\".\nMW-2238 | Component: C-2201 edge-cdn | Window: 18 Sep 2026 02:00\u201302:30 UTC | Purpose: certificate bundle update by Fastwave | Expected alerts: origin 5xx spike | Status: Scheduled.\nMW-2240 | Component: C-3050 events-kafka | Window: 20 Sep 2026 | Status: Scheduled.\n\n=== PAGERRELAY CONFIG STATUS ===\nLast configuration sync from change calendar: 17 Sep 2026 22:00 UTC (daily sync). Next sync: 18 Sep 2026 22:00 UTC.\n\n=== ALERT STREAM (UTC) ===\n00:58 ALR-5580 comp_id C-4100 checkout-svc \u2014 pod restart count 3 in 10 min (warning; below paging threshold). PagerRelay: ticket.\n01:14 ALR-5588 comp_id C-1010 orders-db-ro-analytics \u2014 replica lag 95 s (warning). PagerRelay: ticket.\n01:22 ALR-5591 comp_id C-1004 orders-db-replica-2 \u2014 replica lag above 300 s; values over the last 7 minutes (evaluated every 30 s): min 318 s, max 544 s, current 540 s. Condition sustained 7 min 0 s. PagerRelay decision: SUPPRESS (MW-2231, expected alert \"replica lag\").\n01:23 ALR-5592 comp_id C-9001 repo-scanner \u2014 finding: string matching cloud access key pattern in repo pelagos/checkout-svc, path tests/fixtures/fake_creds.json; fingerprint KTF-00417 (present on the Known Test Fixtures allowlist). PagerRelay: ticket.\n01:24 Router note (night router J. Umeadi): \"Lag on replica-2 looks like a heavy analytics query from svc-reporting that got pointed at replica-2 instead of the DataHarbor analytics replica. PagerRelay suppressed it because of the upgrade window.\"\n01:26 ALR-5595 comp_id C-2201 edge-cdn \u2014 origin 5xx 0.4% (below threshold). PagerRelay: ticket.\n01:27 Chat from on-call platform engineer (not paged): \"If this is a query from a service account hitting the wrong box, isn't that something for Security? Also the secrets scanner just fired at the same time.\"\n01:28 Router: \"Deciding final destination for ALR-5591 now.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8670357465744019, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-long_policy-19", "item_sha256": "d01a4b65d772cca6216d1e524c6a59919ef722c0c1ea1fc41154f4e0df37bf2a", "latency_ms": 417.0470470562577, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.13296423852443695, "yes": 0.8670357465744019}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "BRACKWATER BUILDING SOCIETY \u2014 COLLECTIONS & RECOVERIES\nPAYMENT RELIEF POLICY PR-5 (EXTRACT), AMENDMENT PR-5/A1, AND APPLICATION FILE PRA-2026-02917\n\n=== PAYMENT RELIEF POLICY PR-5 (VERSION 3, 1 FEBRUARY 2025) ===\n\n1. Purpose\nPR-5 sets the conditions under which Brackwater offers a temporary Payment Relief Plan (reduced monthly payments for up to six months, with the shortfall added to the balance) to residential mortgage customers in financial difficulty. Customers who do not qualify are still offered the standard arrears support options in policy PR-2 (payment arrangements, term extensions, referral to free debt advice). PR-5 decisions must be consistent and evidence-based; case handlers may not waive any eligibility condition.\n\n2. Definitions\n2.1 \"Household\" \u2014 the borrowers named on the mortgage and their spouses or partners living at the mortgaged property.\n2.2 \"Gross Monthly Income\" \u2014 the total income of the Household before tax and deductions in a calendar month, including salary, wages, self-employed drawings, pensions and regular benefits. It excludes one-off payments: annual or discretionary bonuses, redundancy lump sums, gifts, inheritances, and tax refunds.\n2.3 \"Hardship Event\" \u2014 a change of circumstances that reduces Household income, such as job loss, reduction of hours, illness, bereavement or relationship breakdown. The \"Hardship Month\" is the calendar month in which the Hardship Event occurred.\n2.4 \"Baseline Income\" \u2014 the average Gross Monthly Income over the full calendar months immediately before the Hardship Month (the number of months is set in clause 3.1(a)).\n2.5 \"Current Income\" \u2014 the average Gross Monthly Income over the two most recent full calendar months before the application date.\n2.6 \"Days Past Due\" \u2014 the number of days since the oldest unpaid contractual monthly payment fell due, on the application date.\n2.7 \"Application date\" \u2014 the date on which a complete application (form PR-5A plus evidence) is received.\n\n3. Eligibility \u2014 all conditions must be met\n3.1 (a) Qualifying hardship: Current Income is at least 25% lower than Baseline Income. Baseline Income is calculated over the 12 full calendar months before the Hardship Month.\n    (b) Arrears: Days Past Due is 60 or fewer.\n    (c) Frequency: no Payment Relief Plan has started in the 24 months before the application date. The 24 months are measured from the start date of the previous plan, not its end date.\n    (d) Property: the mortgaged property is the Household's owner-occupied main residence. Buy-to-let and second homes are excluded.\n    (e) Affordability: the reduced payment proposed by the customer is at least 50% of the contractual payment.\n3.2 Where a condition is not met, the case handler records the condition and offers PR-2 options.\n\n4. Plan terms\n4.1 Maximum duration six months; review at month three.\n4.2 Interest continues to accrue. The shortfall is capitalised at the end of the plan unless the customer repays it earlier.\n4.3 Credit reporting: plans are reported as an \"arrangement to pay\" to credit reference agencies.\n\n5. Evidence\n5.1 Payslips or employer letters for each employed member of the Household covering the Baseline and Current periods; bank statements for the last three months; for self-employed customers, management accounts or bank statements.\n5.2 Handlers must use gross figures from payslips. Net pay figures from bank statements are used only to check that the payslips are consistent.\n\n6. Vulnerable customers\n6.1 Customers showing signs of vulnerability are referred to the Specialist Support team, which may offer additional forbearance under policy PR-9. Referral does not change PR-5 eligibility.\n\n7. Decisions and communication\n7.1 Decisions are made within 10 business days of the application date. The customer receives a written decision explaining which conditions were met or not met.\n7.2 Declined customers may ask for a review by a senior handler within 30 days; the review applies the same conditions.\n7.3 Collection activity (letters, calls) is paused while an application is being assessed, but arrears continue to be reported.\n\n8. Related policies (summary)\nPR-2 Arrears support: payment arrangements of up to 12 months to clear arrears; temporary interest-only switch for up to 6 months; term extension subject to affordability; referral to free debt advice charities.\nPR-9 Specialist support: additional flexibility for customers with serious illness, bereavement or domestic abuse, decided by the Specialist Support team.\nPR-12 Litigation: the conditions under which Brackwater issues possession proceedings (never within 6 months of the first missed payment and never while an application under PR-5 or PR-9 is being assessed).\n\n9. Record keeping\nAll calculations must be recorded in the case management system with the monthly figures used, so that the decision can be audited. Quality assurance samples 10% of decisions each month.\n\n=== AMENDMENT PR-5/A1 (EFFECTIVE 1 JULY 2026, FOR APPLICATIONS RECEIVED FROM THAT DATE) ===\n(1) In clause 3.1(a), \"12 full calendar months\" is replaced by \"6 full calendar months\".\n(2) The 25% threshold and the definition of Gross Monthly Income (including the exclusion of one-off payments) are unchanged.\n(3) New clause 3.1(f): customers with an active County Court claim must be referred to Litigation before a plan is offered. [Not applicable in this file \u2014 no claim issued.]\n\n=== APPLICATION FILE PRA-2026-02917 ===\nBorrowers: Mr Callum Deverell and Ms Anya Deverell (joint mortgage).\nProperty: 19 Heron Walk, Brackwater \u2014 owner-occupied main residence since 2018.\nMortgage balance: GBP 213,400. Product: 5-year fixed rate 4.19% until 31 May 2028, early repayment charge 3%. Remaining term: 21 years 4 months. Loan-to-value: 61% (desktop valuation, January 2026). Contractual monthly payment: GBP 1,286.\nApplication date: 10 September 2026 (form PR-5A and full evidence received).\nProposed reduced payment: GBP 700 per month for six months.\n\nHardship Event: Ms Deverell's employer reduced her contract from 37.5 to 22.5 hours per week with effect from 1 March 2026 (employer letter dated 12 February 2026). Hardship Month: March 2026.\n\nHousehold gross income (from payslips), by month:\n- September 2025: GBP 6,200\n- October 2025: GBP 6,200\n- November 2025: GBP 6,200\n- December 2025: GBP 9,200 (includes Mr Deverell's annual performance bonus of GBP 3,000, shown separately on the payslip as \"Annual bonus\")\n- January 2026: GBP 6,200\n- February 2026: GBP 6,200\n- March 2026: GBP 5,050\n- April 2026: GBP 4,960\n- May 2026: GBP 4,900\n- June 2026: GBP 4,850\n- July 2026: GBP 4,780\n- August 2026: GBP 4,760\n\nHousehold net pay received in bank account (for consistency check only): September 2025 \u2013 February 2026 average GBP 4,650 (excluding December bonus); July\u2013August 2026 average GBP 3,390.\n\nArrears: payments due 15 July 2026 and 15 August 2026 missed; the 15 July payment is the oldest unpaid payment. Days Past Due on 10 September 2026: 57.\n\nOther commitments (from credit file): car finance GBP 289 per month; two credit cards with total balance GBP 3,120, minimum payments up to date. No County Court judgments.\nVulnerability screening: no indicators recorded; customer declined referral to debt advice for now.\nPrevious forbearance: Payment Relief Plan PRP-2024-0551, started 1 June 2024, ended 30 November 2024; completed successfully.\n\nCustomer's covering letter: \"Our take-home pay has dropped by more than a quarter \u2014 from about \u00a34,650 a month to about \u00a33,390. We are behind by two payments and want to get back on track. We had a plan in 2024 but that ended almost two years ago.\"\n\nHandler's draft calculation (first line, 11 September 2026):\n\"Baseline (Sep\u2013Feb, 6 months): total 40,200 / 6 = 6,700. Current (Jul\u2013Aug): 4,770. Drop = 28.8% \u2014 over 25%. Net drop 27.1% also over 25%. DPD 57 (OK). Previous plan started June 2024 \u2014 more than 24 months ago (OK). Owner-occupied (OK). Proposed 700 is 54% of 1,286 (OK). Recommend approve.\"\nTeam leader note: \"Please double-check the income definitions before approval.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.562176525592804, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-probability-03", "item_sha256": "82519706bb963b6333651d12845b6ee22ba182c25a68288c8d349947ea4fdf13", "latency_ms": 97.27381262928247, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.43782350420951843, "yes": 0.562176525592804}, "source_split": "public:probability", "state": "KORRIDAN ELECTRONICS \u2014 INCOMING INSPECTION, LOT PSU-2609-07 (12 POWER SUPPLY UNITS)\n\nSupplier certificate: the supplier's own end-of-line test found that exactly 3 of the 12 units in this lot are defective (output ripple above specification). The defective units were not marked and are indistinguishable from good units without testing. Korridan has verified that the supplier's count is exact.\n\nSampling plan SP-4 (version 2): the inspector draws 2 units at random, without replacement, and tests them.\nSampling plan SP-4 (version 3, in force from 1 September 2026, supersedes version 2): the inspector draws 3 units at random, without replacement, and tests them. The lot is rejected if any tested unit is defective.\n\nOther lots received the same day (for information): PSU-2609-05 (20 units, supplier reports 0 defective, accepted after sampling), PSU-2609-06 (8 units, supplier reports 1 defective, sampling pending).\nTest procedure: each sampled unit is load-tested for 15 minutes; a unit whose ripple exceeds specification is classified defective. The test is assumed to be perfectly accurate for this purpose.\nInspection date: 18 September 2026. The inspector follows the plan in force. No unit has been tested yet.\nInspector's note: \"With a quarter of the lot defective, chance of catching one is under 50% \u2014 probably passes.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8001413345336914, "correct": false, "expected": "on_time", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-probability-04", "item_sha256": "b1af8dc4dc43de1f2b0d55f4e87d943da9ffe310739f3d500f7364a16952a4bc", "latency_ms": 170.1725823804736, "options": ["early", "on_time", "late"], "prediction": "late", "probabilities": {"early": 0.02132304385304451, "late": 0.8001413345336914, "on_time": 0.17853567004203796}, "source_split": "public:probability", "state": "OSTERLAND GARDEN SUPPLY \u2014 INBOUND LOGISTICS: DELIVERY FORECAST FOR SHIPMENT SH-2026-3391\n\nShipment SH-2026-3391: Brisa Freight, service Express, pickup week 39/2026, route Porto \u2192 Hanover.\nOutcome categories: early (delivered before the promised day), on time (on the promised day), late (after the promised day).\nMethod (logistics planning handbook): estimate from past shipments on the same route with the same carrier and the same service level, excluding shipments picked up in peak weeks 47\u201352, because peak-season congestion is not representative of the rest of the year. Use the frequencies directly.\n\nPast shipments on route Porto \u2192 Hanover, 2025 (all 30):\nSH-4401 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 49/2025 | late\nSH-4402 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 32/2025 | early\nSH-4403 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 37/2025 | on time\nSH-4404 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 19/2025 | late\nSH-4405 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 35/2025 | late\nSH-4406 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 52/2025 | late\nSH-4407 | Brisa Freight | Economy | pickup week 38/2025 | on time\nSH-4408 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 29/2025 | on time\nSH-4409 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 25/2025 | late\nSH-4410 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 48/2025 | late\nSH-4411 | Brisa Freight | Economy | pickup week 44/2025 | late\nSH-4412 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 31/2025 | early\nSH-4413 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 19/2025 | on time\nSH-4414 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 33/2025 | late\nSH-4415 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 14/2025 | on time\nSH-4416 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 49/2025 | late\nSH-4417 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 33/2025 | on time\nSH-4418 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 26/2025 | on time\nSH-4419 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 50/2025 | late\nSH-4420 | Kolt Logistics | Express | pickup week 41/2025 | early\nSH-4421 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 32/2025 | early\nSH-4422 | Brisa Freight | Economy | pickup week 41/2025 | late\nSH-4423 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 42/2025 | on time\nSH-4424 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 24/2025 | on time\nSH-4425 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 48/2025 | on time\nSH-4426 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 33/2025 | late\nSH-4427 | Brisa Freight | Economy | pickup week 30/2025 | late\nSH-4428 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 16/2025 | on time\nSH-4429 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 19/2025 | on time\nSH-4430 | Brisa Freight | Express | pickup week 16/2025 | on time\n\nPlanner's comment: \"Brisa is late a lot \u2014 I count 12 late out of their 24 shipments.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.5527921915054321, "correct": false, "expected": "escalated", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-probability-07", "item_sha256": "cc6548c80ce8e0f88cb823d84a8929d02c5a89899e5bb0bfff3c292c0a124e64", "latency_ms": 176.77474673837423, "options": ["resolved_first_contact", "escalated", "reopened"], "prediction": "resolved_first_contact", "probabilities": {"escalated": 0.43051499128341675, "reopened": 0.0166928768157959, "resolved_first_contact": 0.5527921915054321}, "source_split": "public:probability", "state": "LUMINARC SOFTWARE \u2014 SUPPORT OPERATIONS: OUTCOME ESTIMATE FOR TICKET T-58741\n\nBackground: identity-provider connector release 5.2, deployed on 15 July 2026, fixed the root cause of most SSO login failures, so tickets opened on or before 15 July are not comparable with later ones. Enterprise and Standard tiers have different entitlements (Enterprise tickets go to a dedicated team with direct access to engineering).\nEstimation rule (support analytics): use past tickets with the same tier and the same category that were opened after 15 July 2026; outcome frequencies among them are the estimate.\nOutcomes: resolved first contact (closed by the first responder without escalation); escalated (handed to engineering); reopened (closed, then reopened by the customer within 7 days).\n\nNew ticket T-58741: Enterprise tier, category SSO login, opened 18 September 2026.\n\nPast tickets (last 30, SSO login and Billing):\nT-58003 | Standard | SSO login | opened 2026-08-27 | resolved first contact\nT-58006 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-07-16 | escalated\nT-58009 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-05-19 | reopened\nT-58012 | Standard | Billing | opened 2026-07-16 | escalated\nT-58015 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-09-09 | escalated\nT-58018 | Standard | Billing | opened 2026-07-14 | resolved first contact\nT-58021 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-07-30 | escalated\nT-58024 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-08-11 | resolved first contact\nT-58027 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-06-17 | escalated\nT-58030 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-07-30 | escalated\nT-58033 | Standard | Billing | opened 2026-06-30 | resolved first contact\nT-58036 | Standard | SSO login | opened 2026-07-22 | escalated\nT-58039 | Enterprise | Billing | opened 2026-08-11 | reopened\nT-58042 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-06-30 | escalated\nT-58045 | Enterprise | Billing | opened 2026-07-30 | resolved first contact\nT-58048 | Enterprise | Billing | opened 2026-07-22 | resolved first contact\nT-58051 | Standard | SSO login | opened 2026-08-04 | resolved first contact\nT-58054 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-08-19 | escalated\nT-58057 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-05-06 | reopened\nT-58060 | Enterprise | Billing | opened 2026-09-09 | resolved first contact\nT-58063 | Standard | SSO login | opened 2026-07-22 | resolved first contact\nT-58066 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-07-16 | reopened\nT-58069 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-08-04 | escalated\nT-58072 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-06-17 | reopened\nT-58075 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-09-09 | resolved first contact\nT-58078 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-08-27 | resolved first contact\nT-58081 | Standard | SSO login | opened 2026-08-19 | resolved first contact\nT-58084 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-06-17 | resolved first contact\nT-58087 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-09-02 | escalated\nT-58090 | Enterprise | SSO login | opened 2026-08-19 | escalated\n\nSupport lead's remark: \"Most SSO tickets get solved on first contact nowadays.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.531209409236908, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-probability-08", "item_sha256": "530c35b831fe041f979fa47b14027469bd39386aec22b97d919b39e7d4b0710d", "latency_ms": 87.11839094758034, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.531209409236908, "yes": 0.4687906503677368}, "source_split": "public:probability", "state": "TRAVEL DESK NOTE \u2014 CONNECTION RISK FOR PASSENGER N. ABERNATHY, 25 SEPTEMBER 2026\n\nItinerary: inbound flight SK 1462 scheduled to arrive at the hub at 14:10; outbound flight SK 2019 scheduled to depart at 15:20. Minimum connection time at the hub: 30 minutes.\nConnection rule (from the hub's transfer data): with the outbound on time, the passenger makes the connection if the inbound arrives no more than 40 minutes late. If the outbound departs at least 20 minutes late, the passenger makes it if the inbound arrives no more than 60 minutes late. The outbound never departs early.\nEvidence:\n- Inbound SK 1462, last 50 operations on this weekday and season: 34 arrived no more than 40 minutes late; 6 arrived between 41 and 60 minutes late; 10 arrived more than 60 minutes late.\n- Outbound SK 2019: departs at least 20 minutes late on 25% of days, otherwise on time (within 5 minutes).\n- The two flights' delays are independent (different aircraft, crews and origins).\nTravel agent's note: \"34 out of 50 on-time inbounds \u2014 so about a one-in-three chance of misconnecting.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.9669140577316284, "correct": false, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-temporal_numeric-03", "item_sha256": "bdae735f901c4d18d8255909a191f0ed2f015fe89f3da7f9edf700e2b5f89a58", "latency_ms": 97.25566580891609, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.9669140577316284, "yes": 0.03308597952127457}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": "BRIGHTLINE HOME APPLIANCES \u2014 EXTENDED WARRANTY CERTIFICATE EW-5540981 (EXTRACT) AND CLAIM RECORD\n\nProduct: Brightline FrostPro 70 fridge-freezer, serial FP70-2208-11934.\nDelivery date: 31 August 2026.\nTerm. This extended warranty covers breakdowns reported during the period that starts on the delivery date and ends 18 months later. Where the calendar month in which the period ends has no day with the same number as the delivery day, the period ends on the last day of that month. The period ends at 24:00 on its last day.\nTime reference. All times are determined in Brightline's local time at its claims centre in Rotterdam, Central European Time (UTC+1 in winter). Claims submitted online are time-stamped in the customer's local time and must be converted.\nClaims. A claim is \"reported\" when the online claim form is submitted or a phone call to the claims line begins.\nExclusions (extract). Cosmetic damage; consumables such as light bulbs and water filters; damage from power surges unless a surge protector was used.\n\nClaim record CL-28-00471\nCustomer location: Halifax, Nova Scotia. Customer's local time zone on the submission date: UTC\u22124.\nOnline claim form submitted: 29 February 2028 at 18:30 customer local time.\nFault: compressor does not start; freezer compartment at +12 \u00b0C.\nClaims centre note (1 March 2028): \"18 months from 31 August 2026 lands on 28 February 2028, so the cover ended the day before this claim. Recommend decline.\"\nPrevious claims: none.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.9241418242454529, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-temporal_numeric-06", "item_sha256": "b3fec91be9fbb2ebb661a0f55bc1b3024944cef117482672718c403c9c5ebfcf", "latency_ms": 99.81485456228256, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.07585817575454712, "yes": 0.9241418242454529}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": "AIRCARGO LOAD CONTROL \u2014 POSITION 22P (MAIN DECK), FLIGHT KX 418, 14 SEPTEMBER 2026\n\nLoad rule LR-7: The gross weight of a unit load device (ULD) on main-deck position 22P must not exceed 2,500 kg. Gross weight = net cargo weight + ULD tare weight + restraint equipment (straps, nets, edge protectors) weight. Weights are converted to kilograms using 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg and the total is then rounded up to the next whole kilogram before comparison.\nLoad rule LR-9 (for information): contoured positions 11L\u201314R have a limit of 3,000 kg; lower-deck positions have a limit of 1,588 kg.\n\nULD PMC 44817 KX \u2014 build-up sheet:\n- ULD type: PMC pallet, tare weight 105 kg (stencilled on the pallet).\n- Net cargo (shipper's declaration, AWB 176-5520 3381): 5,280 lb, machine parts in crates.\n- Restraint equipment actually used (warehouse scan): 1 pallet net 9.5 kg; 4 ratchet straps at 0.6 kg each; edge protectors 0.2 kg total. Restraint total 12.1 kg.\nBuild-up supervisor note: \"5,280 lb is about 2,395 kg, plus 105 tare = 2,500 kg. Right on the limit, which is allowed \u2014 load it on 22P.\"\nOther positions (for information): 21P gross 2,230 kg; 23P gross 2,480 kg.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.6426923274993896, "correct": false, "expected": "sep_26", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-temporal_numeric-07", "item_sha256": "f1b05f21d6623d3114c387170006db1ee9682195ac1c9eec2af67dfa39ec991d", "latency_ms": 200.71840472519398, "options": ["sep_24", "sep_25", "sep_26", "sep_27", "sep_28"], "prediction": "sep_24", "probabilities": {"sep_24": 0.6426923274993896, "sep_25": 0.12655360996723175, "sep_26": 0.16249807178974152, "sep_27": 0.03625822067260742, "sep_28": 0.031997766345739365}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": "NIMBUSGRID CLOUD \u2014 BUDGET ALERT CONFIGURATION AND DAILY COST EXPORT (ACCOUNT ANALYTICS-PROD, SEPTEMBER 2026, USD)\n\nBudget BGT-ANALYTICS-09: monthly budget USD 12,000.00.\nAlert rule A1: \"Fires once, at the end of the first day on which the month-to-date net cost is at or above 80% of the monthly budget.\"\nDefinitions (NimbusGrid billing guide): \"Net cost\" = usage charges minus credits applied, excluding taxes. Taxes are shown for information and are never counted toward budgets. Credits are counted on the day they are applied.\nNote: a promotional credit of USD 600.00 was applied on 18 September (migration incentive).\n\nDaily export:\nSep 01 | usage 402.10 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.17\nSep 02 | usage 389.55 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.16\nSep 03 | usage 415.00 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 33.20\nSep 04 | usage 398.40 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.87\nSep 05 | usage 377.25 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 30.18\nSep 06 | usage 361.80 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 28.94\nSep 07 | usage 420.65 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 33.65\nSep 08 | usage 433.10 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 34.65\nSep 09 | usage 409.95 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.80\nSep 10 | usage 395.00 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.60\nSep 11 | usage 388.70 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.10\nSep 12 | usage 402.35 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.19\nSep 13 | usage 371.20 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 29.70\nSep 14 | usage 366.45 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 29.32\nSep 15 | usage 418.90 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 33.51\nSep 16 | usage 427.30 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 34.18\nSep 17 | usage 411.05 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.88\nSep 18 | usage 399.60 | credits 600.00 | tax (8%) 31.97\nSep 19 | usage 404.25 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.34\nSep 20 | usage 392.80 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.42\nSep 21 | usage 386.15 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 30.89\nSep 22 | usage 375.40 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 30.03\nSep 23 | usage 410.00 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.80\nSep 24 | usage 421.75 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 33.74\nSep 25 | usage 398.00 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.84\nSep 26 | usage 223.30 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 17.86\nSep 27 | usage 380.00 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 30.40\nSep 28 | usage 412.60 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 33.01\nSep 29 | usage 405.10 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 32.41\nSep 30 | usage 399.85 | credits 0.00 | tax (8%) 31.99\n\nFinOps channel, 24 September: \"With tax we're already over 9,600 \u2014 the alert should have fired today. Something's broken?\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.9324532747268677, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-temporal_numeric-09", "item_sha256": "6eb4e620d945c2d5dbcbc8b1d84573ffad7aa7b1ce05e923b304e509e27958ee", "latency_ms": 85.0509013980627, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.06754669547080994, "yes": 0.9324532747268677}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": "PATIENT MEDICATION GUIDANCE \u2014 TACROVEX (FICTIONAL IMMUNOSUPPRESSANT), TWICE-DAILY DOSING \u2014 EXTRACT, AND TRAVEL DIARY\n\nDosing rule: Take one dose every 12 hours. A dose counts as \"on schedule\" if it is taken no earlier than 11 hours and no later than 13 hours after the previous dose, measured in actual elapsed time. When travelling across time zones, do not simply follow the clock of your destination; calculate elapsed time.\nIf a dose is outside the window, contact the transplant clinic's 24-hour line for advice before the following dose.\n\nTime zones for this trip: Chicago on 3 September 2026: UTC\u22125 (CDT). Madrid on 3\u20134 September 2026: UTC+2 (CEST).\n\nPatient travel diary (R. Castellano-Wu):\n- 3 Sep, 13:00 Chicago time: took dose (lunch, at home).\n- 3 Sep, 17:30 Chicago time: flight departed O'Hare for Madrid.\n- On boarding I set my watch to Madrid time.\n- 4 Sep, 01:30 by my watch (Madrid time), on the plane: took the next dose. \"That's 12 and a half hours after my lunchtime dose \u2014 within the window.\"\n- 4 Sep, 08:55 Madrid time: landed Madrid.\n- 4 Sep, 13:30 Madrid time: took dose at hotel.\nOther notes: seat 32C; meal service at 19:00 Chicago time.\nClinic information sheet (extract): \"Missing a dose or taking two doses too close together can change blood levels. Keep a written log of dose times in one time zone (for example your home time zone) while travelling.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.4652274250984192, "correct": false, "expected": "1", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-a-temporal_numeric-12", "item_sha256": "6ead80aad374fdad4f14be0afcc203e252f31735d4f78cd985e6ed0170e02473", "latency_ms": 91.56844299286604, "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "prediction": "3", "probabilities": {"0": 0.09160871058702469, "1": 0.08084440231323242, "2": 0.3623194992542267, "3": 0.4652274250984192}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": "REGIONAL AMBULANCE SERVICE \u2014 WORKING TIME RULE WT-2 AND ROSTER EXTRACT (PARAMEDIC T. VOLKOV)\n\nWT-2 Daily rest: between the end of one shift and the start of the next, a paramedic must have at least 11 consecutive hours of rest, measured in actual elapsed time. A rest period of exactly 11 hours complies. Each rest period shorter than 11 hours counts as one violation.\nClock change: on Sunday 25 October 2026 at 03:00 local summer time (CEST, UTC+2), clocks go back to 02:00 local winter time (CET, UTC+1). The roster system shows local wall-clock times only.\n\nRoster (local times):\nS1 Wed 21 Oct 13:00 \u2013 21:00\nS2 Thu 22 Oct 07:30 \u2013 15:30\nS3 Fri 23 Oct 06:00 \u2013 14:00\nS4 Sat 24 Oct 10:00 \u2013 18:00\nS5 Sun 25 Oct 04:30 \u2013 12:30\nS6 Sun 25 Oct 23:30 \u2013 Mon 26 Oct 07:30\nS7 Mon 26 Oct 18:30 \u2013 Tue 27 Oct 02:30\nS8 Tue 27 Oct 13:30 \u2013 21:30\nOther WT-2 provisions (for information): maximum shift length 12 hours; at least one 24-hour rest in every 7 days; night-shift allowance for hours between 23:00 and 06:00 local time.\nRest periods are measured from the rostered end of one shift to the rostered start of the next; overruns are recorded separately and are not part of this review.\nUnion rep's note: \"By the roster clock I see four problem rests: S1\u2192S2, S4\u2192S5, and S5\u2192S6 and S6\u2192S7, which are both only 11 hours \u2014 so four violations.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8149234652519226, "correct": false, "expected": "real_incident", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-02", "item_sha256": "d6f66f9ceb196f4af8e4a37644bea3f35b88fe83589c738f3db17a46f3c77f46", "latency_ms": 103.84051036089659, "options": ["cannot_determine", "real_incident", "false_positive"], "prediction": "false_positive", "probabilities": {"cannot_determine": 0.024608558043837547, "false_positive": 0.8149234652519226, "real_incident": 0.1604679375886917}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "ALERT  pg-disk-high   severity: page   fired 2026-09-19 04:12 UTC\n  host: db-03.prod   mount: /var/lib/postgresql   used: 97.2 %   threshold: 95 %   source: node-alertd\n\nOn-call chat (#sre-oncall)\n  04:14  Ravi: paged for disk on db-03. Opened the \"DB Fleet\" Grafana dashboard \u2014 db-03 disk panel says 61 %. Flat line all night. Looks like a false positive?\n  04:16  Ravi: node-alertd has been flaky before (it misfired twice in August on cache-07).\n  04:17  Mei: Could be. The dashboard is our source of truth usually.\n\nRUNBOOK RB-DB-12 \"Disk alerts on database hosts\" (last reviewed 2026-08-20)\n  1. Metric sources. The \"DB Fleet\" Grafana dashboard is fed by metrics-agent v1, which only collects the ROOT volume (\"/\") of each host. Data volumes (/var/lib/postgresql) are monitored exclusively by node-alertd, and no dashboard panel shows them yet (ticket OBS-440).\n  2. Known false positives: the August cache-07 misfires were caused by node-alertd reading a tmpfs mount; fixed in node-alertd 2.3.1 (rolled out to all hosts 2026-08-25).\n  3. If the alert reports a data volume above threshold, treat it as real: start WAL/archive cleanup steps in \u00a74.\n\nHost facts: db-03 \u2014 root volume \"/\" 40 GB; data volume /var/lib/postgresql 2 TB (separate disk). node-alertd version on db-03: 2.3.1.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.5922008156776428, "correct": false, "expected": "time_barred", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-03", "item_sha256": "586d6b1a075971595a9abd906f1369c4d54701b187521dda270cc16381924eaf", "latency_ms": 105.6307153776288, "options": ["within_limitation", "time_barred", "insufficient_information"], "prediction": "within_limitation", "probabilities": {"insufficient_information": 0.3169822692871094, "time_barred": 0.0908169373869896, "within_limitation": 0.5922008156776428}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "RIVERBEND LEGAL AID CLINIC \u2014 INTAKE SCREENING NOTE\n\nScreening rule SR-3 (limitation, property-damage claims against contractors):\n  A claim is time-barred if it is filed more than 3 years after the date on which the claimant knew, or reasonably should have known, of the damage. No other tolling rule applies to this claim type in this jurisdiction. If the knowledge date cannot be pinned down, use the LATEST date consistent with the claimant's own account (most favourable to the claimant). Screeners mark a case \"insufficient_information\" only if even that latest possible date cannot be bounded.\n\nIntake interview, 2026-09-16 (screener: T. Alvarez)\n  Client: Noor Haddad. Opponent: DryWell Roofing LLC (roof replacement completed 2022-11-08).\n  Q: When did you first notice the water damage?\n  A: \"Honestly I don't remember the exact day. It was after Easter 2023 \u2014 we'd had guests over for Easter and the ceiling was fine then. And I know it was before my birthday, May 29th, because my sister saw the stain when she came for my birthday dinner and told me I had to call someone... which I only did in the autumn.\"\n  Q: Any documents?\n  A: \"The first quote from another roofer is from October 2023.\"\n  Planned filing date if we take the case: 2026-09-25.\nScreener remark: \"Exact knowledge date unknown \u2014 client can't recall. Flag as insufficient info?\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8595718145370483, "correct": false, "expected": "within_policy", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-07", "item_sha256": "a69a5055591a4c05361fe5f04bd069cfb5ca5932467367288e21ce8fd52ca7df", "latency_ms": 103.61473821103573, "options": ["within_policy", "over_limit", "insufficient_information"], "prediction": "over_limit", "probabilities": {"insufficient_information": 0.10266122221946716, "over_limit": 0.8595718145370483, "within_policy": 0.0377669520676136}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "VANTOR CONSULTING \u2014 EXPENSE POLICY EP-3 (meals)\n  - Client meals are reimbursable up to EUR 60.00 per attendee (all attendees, including employees), inclusive of drinks and tip.\n  - Alcohol is reimbursable within that limit for client meals.\n  - The number of attendees is taken from the attendee list in the expense report or the linked calendar entry; the receipt alone is not required to show it.\n  - An expense report can be decided when the receipt total and the attendee count are known.\n\nEXPENSE REPORT ER-26-0913 (submitted by Lena Hartwig, Senior Consultant)\n  Line 1: Client dinner, Restaurant Zur Linde, Stuttgart, 2026-09-10. Amount EUR 174.00. Category: client meal. Comment: \"Dinner after kickoff workshop, see calendar.\"\n  Receipt (itemised):\n    3 x Tagesmen\u00fc              @ 48.00   144.00\n    3 x Mineralwasser 0.75l    @  5.00    15.00\n    3 x Espresso               @  5.00    15.00\n    Total                               174.00   (tip: none)\n  Linked calendar entry: \"Kickoff dinner \u2014 Zur Linde 19:30\". Attendees: Lena Hartwig; Jorge Ortega (Klient, Brenner AG); Katrin Fiedler (Klient, Brenner AG).\nApprover (manager) comment: \"EUR 174 for a dinner is way over the EUR 60 limit. Also no attendee count on the expense line \u2014 can't judge. Sending back?\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.405500203371048, "correct": true, "expected": "legitimate", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-09", "item_sha256": "1bfa533a18081763bb13dcd012dd834cd0f95b8331a8995f2e66330d6647f0e7", "latency_ms": 111.04599665850401, "options": ["legitimate", "compromised", "inconclusive"], "prediction": "legitimate", "probabilities": {"compromised": 0.2786959409713745, "inconclusive": 0.3158038854598999, "legitimate": 0.405500203371048}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "SOC TRIAGE \u2014 ALERT IT-5530 \"Impossible travel\" (our corporate identity provider)\nUser: s.petrov@corvid-labs.example (Staff Engineer, based in Berlin office)\n\nSign-in events (UTC), 2026-09-17:\n  07:10  Berlin, DE   IP 203.0.113.24 (Corvid Berlin office egress)  device: BER-DSK-0291 (registered, type: desktop-sync-agent)  event: token_refresh  interactive: false\n  09:05  Lisbon, PT   IP 198.51.100.77 (hotel Wi-Fi, ISP Altice)        device: LT-SP-4410 (registered laptop, assigned to s.petrov)  event: sign_in  interactive: true  MFA: FIDO2 hardware key (phishing-resistant)  result: success\n  09:40  Lisbon, PT   same IP/device  event: sign_in (Jira)  interactive: true  MFA: session\n\nDetection logic note: impossible travel = two sign-ins by one user from locations that could not be travelled between in the elapsed time (Berlin\u2192Lisbon is ~2,300 km; 1 h 55 min elapsed).\n\nTRIAGE RUBRIC SOC-R4\n  - Only INTERACTIVE sign-ins are compared for impossible travel. Non-interactive token refreshes by registered unattended devices (desktop-sync-agent, kiosk, CI runner) are excluded, because they keep running while the user is away.\n  - Classify \"legitimate\" when the remaining interactive sign-ins are geographically consistent AND come from a device registered to the user AND passed phishing-resistant MFA.\n  - Classify \"compromised\" when any interactive sign-in fails these tests and there is no benign explanation in the record.\n  - Classify \"inconclusive\" only when the record lacks data needed to apply these tests.\n\nOther context: HR travel system \u2014 none filed for s.petrov this month. Calendar shows \"OOO\" 2026-09-16 to 2026-09-19, no location given.\nAnalyst draft: \"Berlin 07:10 and Lisbon 09:05 is physically impossible, no travel request filed \u2192 compromised, disable account.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.49810025095939636, "correct": true, "expected": "ask_user_which_alex", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-10", "item_sha256": "47b8ac0ce3a3b24f85691eb36732ebd4f83cecec549aace43193b74c865f81b6", "latency_ms": 98.55368174612522, "options": ["ask_user_which_alex", "send_to_alex_moreno", "send_to_alexandra_lind"], "prediction": "ask_user_which_alex", "probabilities": {"ask_user_which_alex": 0.49810025095939636, "send_to_alex_moreno": 0.2352859079837799, "send_to_alexandra_lind": 0.2666138708591461}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": {"agent_policy": ["Send email only to recipients the user has unambiguously identified.", "If a name the user gives matches more than one contact, ask which one \u2014 unless earlier messages in THIS conversation explicitly identify the person (full name, email address, or unique role).", "Do not guess from the topic of the attachment."], "contacts_matching_Alex": [{"display_name": "Alex Moreno", "email": "a.moreno@fernhill.example", "signature": "Alex", "title": "Finance Business Partner"}, {"display_name": "Alexandra Lind", "email": "a.lind@fernhill.example", "signature": "Alex L.", "title": "Head of Marketing"}], "conversation": [{"role": "user", "text": "Can you finish the Q4 budget summary from the numbers in /shared/budget/q4.xlsx?"}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Done \u2014 the summary is saved as Q4_budget_summary.pdf. It covers all departments, including the marketing campaign line items."}, {"role": "user", "text": "Great. Alex asked for this on Tuesday, the marketing spend part especially. Send it to Alex please."}], "recent_email_threads": [{"date": "Tue", "from": "a.moreno@fernhill.example", "snippet": "Hi, when can I see the Q4 budget summary?", "subject": "Q4 numbers?"}, {"date": "Tue", "from": "a.lind@fernhill.example", "snippet": "Need to know what we have for Q4 campaigns before Friday.", "subject": "Campaign budget"}]}}
{"confidence": 0.5617015957832336, "correct": false, "expected": "cannot_determine", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-11", "item_sha256": "b5fa8d1a9546ca8bbee0d32d81277b8d47c431a85d2ca67b9ea34920498977b5", "latency_ms": 92.51097217202187, "options": ["grade_correct", "grade_should_be_higher", "cannot_determine"], "prediction": "grade_should_be_higher", "probabilities": {"cannot_determine": 0.3406892418861389, "grade_correct": 0.09760909527540207, "grade_should_be_higher": 0.5617015957832336}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "HARTWELL SECONDARY SCHOOL \u2014 CHEM 11 GRADE APPEAL, reviewed by the department head\n\nCourse outline (term 1):\n  - Final percentage = 40 % homework average + 60 % unit exam.\n  - Homework: 5 assignments, each out of 100. The homework average is the mean of all counted assignments.\n  - A missing assignment counts as 0 unless it is EXCUSED; excused assignments are removed from the average.\n  - An assignment is excused only when the Dean's office approves a documented excuse request. While a request is pending, the assignment's treatment is undetermined and no final term grade may be certified.\n  - Letter grades: A \u2265 90.0 %, B \u2265 80.0 %, C \u2265 70.0 %, below 70 = D.\n\nStudent: Priya Raman\nHomework: HW1 95, HW2 92, HW3 missing, HW4 90, HW5 94\nUnit exam: 91\nGradebook (teacher's entry): HW3 = 0 \u2192 homework avg 74.2 \u2192 final 84.28 % \u2192 B\nDean's office log: 2026-09-03 excuse request for HW3 (medical note attached) received from Priya \u2014 status: PENDING REVIEW.\nPriya's appeal: \"HW3 should be excused, I was in hospital. My grade should be an A.\"\nTeacher comment: \"Rules are rules \u2014 no approved excuse in the system, so it's a zero. The B stands.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.5501667261123657, "correct": false, "expected": "cannot_determine", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-ambiguous-13", "item_sha256": "d2e908865bf18bac0f9f4dc8ff30a71a1482e3666238db7c1e977be52a4c8cfd", "latency_ms": 96.71808779239655, "options": ["cannot_determine", "charge_platform_team", "charge_data_team", "split_50_50"], "prediction": "split_50_50", "probabilities": {"cannot_determine": 0.1391039341688156, "charge_data_team": 0.20239505171775818, "charge_platform_team": 0.1083342507481575, "split_50_50": 0.5501667261123657}, "source_split": "public:ambiguous", "state": "QUILLSOFT \u2014 FINOPS COST ALLOCATION STANDARD FA-2\n  A1 Every cloud cost line is charged to the team in its \"cost-center\" tag.\n  A2 Untagged cost in an account with a single owner team is charged to that owner team.\n  A3 Untagged cost in a JOINT account is split between the owner teams using the ratio recorded for that account in the Joint Account Register. The register ratio is the only valid basis; it is not to be replaced by guesses about who created or used the resources.\n  A4 Who launched a resource (creator identity) is never an allocation criterion.\n\nAugust 2026 anomaly report \u2014 account \"sandbox-shared\" (ID 4410-2291-0073)\n  Tagged cost:   cost-center=platform  USD 3,120   |   cost-center=data  USD 2,480\n  UNTAGGED cost: USD 14,200 \u2014 6 x gpu.a100.x8 instances, launched 2026-08-04 by IAM user d.kowalczyk (Data team), terminated 2026-08-22.\n\nAccount registry: sandbox-shared \u2014 owner: \"Platform & Data (joint)\"; created 2025-01-10.\nJoint Account Register (FinOps wiki, last edited 2026-07-02):\n  | Account         | Owner teams       | Split ratio                         |\n  | analytics-joint | Data / ML         | 70 / 30                             |\n  | sandbox-shared  | Platform / Data   | TBD \u2014 pending FinOps review (Q3)    |\n  | edge-lab        | Platform / Edge   | 50 / 50                             |\n\nSlack, #finops: \n  Platform lead: \"Those were Dawid's GPU boxes. Data team pays, obviously.\"\n  Data lead: \"Joint account, joint bill. Just do 50/50 like edge-lab.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8805965185165405, "correct": false, "expected": "tier3_cfo", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-multi_hop-03", "item_sha256": "e07bc5834bf5129dc348f8b30b05ab4b6a0d0528b46326788962dd970ea663f2", "latency_ms": 518.5949960723519, "options": ["tier1_team_lead", "tier2_department_head", "tier3_cfo", "tier4_board"], "prediction": "tier2_department_head", "probabilities": {"tier1_team_lead": 0.0637902021408081, "tier2_department_head": 0.8805965185165405, "tier3_cfo": 0.04967985674738884, "tier4_board": 0.005933412350714207}, "source_split": "public:multi_hop", "state": "ALDERMOOR INDUSTRIES SE \u2014 PROCURE-TO-PAY MANUAL, Chapter 9 \"Invoice approval routing\" (rev. 2026-06)\n\n9.1 Purpose. Every supplier invoice must be approved at the correct tier before payment. The tier depends on (a) the vendor's risk category and (b) the invoice amount in EUR, including aggregation (9.5).\n\n9.2 Identifying the vendor. Invoices are matched to a vendor master record through the trading-name alias list (Annex A). Several vendors have similar names; match on the EXACT trading name printed on the invoice, including the legal-form suffix (AG, GmbH, Ltd \u2026). The vendor ID from Annex A is used for every later step.\n\n9.3 Risk category. Start with the category in the vendor master (Annex B). The category is raised to ELEVATED if either of the following is true:\n    (i) the vendor's payee bank account is held in a country that is not in the EU/EEA list in Annex D;\n    (ii) the vendor was onboarded less than 6 months before the invoice date.\n    A vendor already rated ELEVATED stays ELEVATED. No rule lowers a category.\n\n9.4 Currency. Non-EUR invoices are converted at the reference rate for the invoice date in Annex C. Annex C quotes the amount of FOREIGN currency per 1 EUR, so EUR amount = foreign amount \u00f7 rate. Round to cents.\n\n9.5 Aggregation (anti-splitting). The amount used for routing is the current invoice PLUS all earlier invoices from the SAME vendor ID against the SAME purchase order whose invoice dates fall within the 30 calendar days before the current invoice date. Earlier invoices are converted at the rate for their own invoice date.\n\n9.6 Approval matrix (routing amount in EUR):\n| Risk category | Tier 1 Team lead | Tier 2 Department head | Tier 3 CFO           | Tier 4 Board (Finance Committee) |\n|---------------|------------------|------------------------|----------------------|----------------------------------|\n| STANDARD      | \u2264 10,000         | > 10,000 \u2013 50,000      | > 50,000 \u2013 250,000   | > 250,000                        |\n| ELEVATED      | \u2264 5,000          | > 5,000 \u2013 25,000       | > 25,000 \u2013 100,000   | > 100,000                        |\n\n9.7 Other rules. Capex invoices additionally need the asset register number (does not change the tier). Intercompany invoices (vendor IDs starting with IC-) are routed to Group Accounting and are out of scope of this chapter. Invoices without a PO go to the \"no-PO\" exception queue first.\n\nANNEX A \u2014 TRADING-NAME ALIAS LIST (extract)\n| Trading name on invoice            | Vendor ID | Notes |\n|------------------------------------|-----------|-------|\n| Brightwater Facility Ltd           | V-0733    |       |\n| Nordstern Geb\u00e4udeservice GmbH      | V-0981    | also invoices as \"NSG Service GmbH\" |\n| NSG Service GmbH                   | V-0981    |       |\n| NSG Services AG                    | V-1042    | Swiss affiliate of Nordstern; separate legal entity |\n| Nordstern Services AG              | V-1042    |       |\n| Oberland K\u00e4ltetechnik GmbH         | V-1107    |       |\n| Pelham Industrial Supplies Ltd     | V-0655    |       |\n| Rhein-Ruhr Arbeitsschutz GmbH      | V-0870    |       |\n| Aldermoor Services UK Ltd          | IC-0012   | intercompany |\n| Aldermoor Polska Sp. z o.o.        | IC-0019   | intercompany |\n| Castellan Logistics BV             | V-0912    |       |\n| Castellan Logistik GmbH            | V-0913    | German sister company of V-0912 |\n| Dunmore Precision Tools Ltd        | V-0588    |       |\n| Eastbrook IT Services Ltd          | V-1003    | trades as \"EBIT\" on some invoices |\n| EBIT                               | V-1003    |       |\n| Falkenried Sicherheitsdienst GmbH  | V-0764    |       |\n| Gr\u00fcnwald Catering AG               | V-1011    |       |\n| Gr\u00fcnwald Catering GmbH             | V-0689    | not related to V-1011 |\n\nANNEX B \u2014 VENDOR MASTER (extract)\n| Vendor ID | Legal name                        | Seat           | Payee bank country | Onboarded  | Risk (master) |\n|-----------|-----------------------------------|----------------|--------------------|------------|---------------|\n| V-0655    | Pelham Industrial Supplies Ltd    | Leeds, GB      | GB                 | 2019-03-12 | ELEVATED      |\n| V-0733    | Brightwater Facility Ltd          | Dublin, IE     | IE                 | 2021-10-01 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0870    | Rhein-Ruhr Arbeitsschutz GmbH     | Essen, DE      | DE                 | 2016-01-20 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0981    | Nordstern Geb\u00e4udeservice GmbH     | Hamburg, DE    | DE                 | 2018-07-02 | STANDARD      |\n| V-1042    | Nordstern Services AG             | Z\u00fcrich, CH     | CH                 | 2024-05-06 | STANDARD      |\n| V-1107    | Oberland K\u00e4ltetechnik GmbH        | Rosenheim, DE  | AT                 | 2026-05-18 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0588    | Dunmore Precision Tools Ltd       | Sheffield, GB  | GB                 | 2015-09-01 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0689    | Gr\u00fcnwald Catering GmbH            | Munich, DE     | DE                 | 2017-04-11 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0764    | Falkenried Sicherheitsdienst GmbH | Hamburg, DE    | DE                 | 2020-02-03 | ELEVATED      |\n| V-0912    | Castellan Logistics BV            | Rotterdam, NL  | NL                 | 2022-08-15 | STANDARD      |\n| V-0913    | Castellan Logistik GmbH           | Duisburg, DE   | DE                 | 2022-08-15 | STANDARD      |\n| V-1003    | Eastbrook IT Services Ltd         | Bristol, GB    | IE                 | 2023-11-20 | STANDARD      |\n| V-1011    | Gr\u00fcnwald Catering AG              | Basel, CH      | DE                 | 2025-02-01 | STANDARD      |\n\nANNEX C \u2014 REFERENCE RATES (foreign currency per 1 EUR)\n| Date       | CHF    | GBP    | USD    |\n|------------|--------|--------|--------|\n| 2026-07-30 | 0.9551 | 0.8412 | 1.0890 |\n| 2026-08-14 | 0.9437 | 0.8398 | 1.0934 |\n| 2026-08-28 | 0.9412 | 0.8431 | 1.1012 |\n| 2026-09-01 | 0.9405 | 0.8440 | 1.1020 |\n| 2026-09-15 | 0.9390 | 0.8455 | 1.1047 |\n\nANNEX D \u2014 EU/EEA COUNTRY LIST (ISO codes)\nAT, BE, BG, CY, CZ, DE, DK, EE, ES, FI, FR, GR, HR, HU, IE, IS, IT, LI, LT, LU, LV, MT, NL, NO, PL, PT, RO, SE, SI, SK\n\nANNEX E \u2014 INVOICE LEDGER, PO-7710 \"Facility services Z\u00fcrich office\" (all invoices booked against this PO)\n| Invoice no.   | Trading name on invoice       | Invoice date | Amount       | Status   |\n|---------------|-------------------------------|--------------|--------------|----------|\n| NS-2026-071   | NSG Services AG               | 2026-07-30   | CHF 80,000.00| PAID     |\n| NG-2026-5520  | NSG Service GmbH              | 2026-08-14   | EUR 3,950.00 | PAID     |\n| NS-2026-118   | NSG Services AG               | 2026-08-28   | CHF 5,900.00 | PAID     |\n| NS-2026-131   | NSG Services AG               | 2026-09-15   | CHF 21,400.00| TO ROUTE |\n\nANNEX F \u2014 HELPDESK FAQ (excerpt)\nQ: Can I split an invoice into two to stay under a limit? A: No \u2014 see 9.5; splitting is detected by aggregation.\nQ: Does the tier depend on the cost centre? A: No. Cost centre owners are informed but do not change the tier.\nQ: What if a vendor sends a credit note? A: Credit notes are not aggregated and do not reduce the routing amount.\nQ: Are Swiss vendors treated as EU for routing? A: Only the Annex D list counts.\n\nANNEX G \u2014 WORKFLOW SERVICE LEVELS AND DELEGATION\nG.1 Approvers must act within 3 business days (Tier 1\u20132) or 5 business days (Tier 3). Tier 4 items are collected for the next Finance Committee meeting (monthly, second Tuesday).\nG.2 Delegation during absence is allowed only to a person at the SAME or HIGHER tier and must be recorded in the workflow tool before the absence starts. Delegation never changes the tier an invoice is routed to.\nG.3 Early-payment discounts do not justify skipping a tier. If a discount would be lost, the AP clerk may mark the item \"priority\" in the workflow tool.\nG.4 Approval by e-mail is not valid; approvals are only recorded in the workflow tool.\nG.5 If the routing amount lies exactly on a boundary (e.g. EUR 25,000.00 for an ELEVATED vendor), the LOWER tier applies, consistent with the \"\u2264\" and \"> \u2026 \u2013\" notation in 9.6.\nG.6 Re-routing: if a vendor's risk category changes after routing but before approval, the invoice is re-routed using the new category.\n\nANNEX H \u2014 CHANGE LOG\n2026-06-01  9.3(i) extended from \"outside the EU\" to \"outside the EU/EEA\" (Norway, Iceland, Liechtenstein no longer trigger ELEVATED).\n2026-03-15  9.5 window changed from 60 to 30 calendar days.\n2025-11-01  Tier 4 threshold for STANDARD raised from EUR 200,000 to EUR 250,000.\n2025-07-01  Annex C switched from \"EUR per foreign unit\" to \"foreign units per EUR\" quotation; see 9.4.\n\nCASE: route invoice NS-2026-131 (NSG Services AG, CHF 21,400.00, invoice date 2026-09-15, PO-7710, service invoice, not capex).\nAP clerk's draft: \"NSG = Nordstern, standard risk, ~EUR 22.8k \u2192 Tier 2 department head.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.4371468126773834, "correct": false, "expected": "bjorn", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-multi_hop-04", "item_sha256": "cd632011f238eb883382e509aa8b88a632e4b88cdb56b2415ab44d96f3a7a5c2", "latency_ms": 411.52359638363123, "options": ["no_page_ticket_only", "ana", "bjorn", "chen", "dmitri", "esra"], "prediction": "dmitri", "probabilities": {"ana": 0.11052802950143814, "bjorn": 0.1822299063205719, "chen": 0.07596471905708313, "dmitri": 0.4371468126773834, "esra": 0.05220974236726761, "no_page_ticket_only": 0.1419207900762558}, "source_split": "public:multi_hop", "state": "FERNWAY PAY \u2014 ON-CALL ROUTING HANDBOOK (SRE), version 14 \u2014 last edited 2026-09-02\n\n=====================================================================\n1. HOW AN ALERT BECOMES A PAGE\n=====================================================================\n1.1 Alerts identify the failing PROCESS and host, not the service. Map the process name to its service using the service catalog (section 3). One service can run several processes.\n1.2 Severity: the alert rule's base severity is adjusted by the service tier (section 2). Only SEV1 and SEV2 page a human. SEV3 and SEV4 create a ticket in the owning team's queue and page nobody, at any time of day.\n1.3 The page goes to the PRIMARY on-call of the team that owns the service. Determine the owning team from the catalog, then check the team directory (section 4) for merges or hand-overs: if a team's services have been transferred, page the receiving team's rotation.\n1.4 Rotations hand over at the times given in section 5. All rotation and override times are in Europe/Berlin local time. Alert timestamps are in UTC. In September 2026 Europe/Berlin is UTC+2 (CEST).\n1.5 Overrides (section 6) replace the scheduled primary for the stated window. An override applies if the alert time falls inside [start, end).\n1.6 Never page a secondary directly; the paging tool escalates to the secondary automatically after 10 minutes without acknowledgement.\n\n=====================================================================\n2. SEVERITY MATRIX\n=====================================================================\n| Alert rule            | Base severity | tier-1 service | tier-2 service | tier-3 service |\n|-----------------------|---------------|----------------|----------------|----------------|\n| ProcessCrashLoop      | SEV3          | raise to SEV2  | stays SEV3     | lower to SEV4  |\n| HighErrorRate5xx      | SEV2          | raise to SEV1  | stays SEV2     | lower to SEV3  |\n| DiskPressure          | SEV3          | stays SEV3     | stays SEV3     | lower to SEV4  |\n| CertExpiry7d          | SEV3          | stays SEV3     | stays SEV3     | stays SEV3     |\n| ReplicationLag        | SEV2          | stays SEV2     | lower to SEV3  | lower to SEV4  |\n| QueueBacklog          | SEV3          | raise to SEV2  | stays SEV3     | lower to SEV4  |\n\n=====================================================================\n3. SERVICE CATALOG (production)\n=====================================================================\n| Service          | Process names             | Owning team     | Tier   |\n|------------------|---------------------------|-----------------|--------|\n| api-gateway      | envoy, gw-sidecar         | Edge            | tier-1 |\n| auth-service     | authd                     | Identity        | tier-1 |\n| billing-export   | billexp                   | Data-Finance    | tier-3 |\n| card-tokenizer   | tokend                    | Payments-Core   | tier-1 |\n| checkout-web     | next-checkout             | Storefront      | tier-2 |\n| fraud-scorer     | fraudd, scorer-py         | Risk            | tier-1 |\n| fx-rates         | fxpoll                    | Payments-Core   | tier-2 |\n| ledger-core      | ledgerd, ledger-migrator  | Payments-Core   | tier-1 |\n| ledger-reports   | ledgerrpt                 | Data-Finance    | tier-3 |\n| notification-hub | notifyd                   | Messaging       | tier-2 |\n| payout-worker    | payoutd                   | Money Movement  | tier-1 |\n| reconciliation   | recond                    | Money Movement  | tier-2 |\n| search-indexer   | idxd                      | Discovery       | tier-3 |\n| session-store    | redis-sess                | Identity        | tier-1 |\n| statement-pdf    | stmtgen                   | Data-Finance    | tier-3 |\n| webhook-relay    | hookd                     | Messaging       | tier-2 |\n\n=====================================================================\n4. TEAM DIRECTORY\n=====================================================================\n- Edge \u2014 rotation \"edge-primary\". Manager: L. Okafor.\n- Identity \u2014 rotation \"identity-primary\". Manager: G. Sandoval.\n- Payments-Core \u2014 MERGED into Money Movement effective 2026-09-01 00:00 Europe/Berlin. All Payments-Core services are now on-call-supported by the Money Movement rotation. The \"payments-core-primary\" rotation is retired and kept read-only for audit; do not page it.\n- Money Movement \u2014 rotation \"mm-primary\" (also covers former Payments-Core services from 2026-09-01). Manager: T. Varga.\n- Risk \u2014 rotation \"risk-primary\". Manager: H. Iqbal.\n- Data-Finance \u2014 business hours only, no pager rotation; tickets only.\n- Messaging, Storefront, Discovery \u2014 rotations \"msg-primary\", \"store-primary\", \"disc-primary\".\n\n=====================================================================\n5. ROTATION SCHEDULES (primary), September 2026\n=====================================================================\nmm-primary \u2014 weekly, hand-over every MONDAY 09:00 Europe/Berlin\n| Week (from Mon 09:00 to next Mon 09:00) | Primary | Secondary |\n|-----------------------------------------|---------|-----------|\n| 2026-08-31 \u2192 2026-09-07                 | Chen    | Esra      |\n| 2026-09-07 \u2192 2026-09-14                 | Esra    | Ana       |\n| 2026-09-14 \u2192 2026-09-21                 | Ana     | Chen      |\n| 2026-09-21 \u2192 2026-09-28                 | Chen    | Esra      |\n\npayments-core-primary (RETIRED 2026-09-01, read-only)\n| Week                                    | Primary | Secondary |\n|-----------------------------------------|---------|-----------|\n| 2026-09-14 \u2192 2026-09-21                 | Dmitri  | Bjorn     |\n| 2026-09-21 \u2192 2026-09-28                 | Bjorn   | Dmitri    |\n\nidentity-primary \u2014 weekly, hand-over Wednesday 10:00: 09-09 \u2192 09-16 Farah; 09-16 \u2192 09-23 Gustav.\nrisk-primary \u2014 weekly, hand-over Monday 09:00: 09-14 \u2192 09-21 Hiro; 09-21 \u2192 09-28 Ines.\nedge-primary \u2014 follow-the-sun (not relevant to Fernway EU services).\n\n=====================================================================\n6. OVERRIDES (all rotations), Europe/Berlin local time\n=====================================================================\n| Rotation         | Start               | End                 | Scheduled primary | Replaced by | Reason |\n|------------------|---------------------|---------------------|-------------------|-------------|--------|\n| mm-primary       | 2026-09-12 20:00    | 2026-09-13 08:00    | Esra              | Ana         | family event |\n| mm-primary       | 2026-09-20 18:00    | 2026-09-21 09:00    | Ana               | Bjorn       | Ana travelling (Bjorn joined mm-primary after the merge) |\n| mm-primary       | 2026-09-21 09:00    | 2026-09-21 14:00    | Chen              | Esra        | Chen at dentist |\n| risk-primary     | 2026-09-21 00:00    | 2026-09-21 12:00    | Hiro              | Ines        | swap |\n| identity-primary | 2026-09-18 18:00    | 2026-09-19 08:00    | Gustav            | Farah       | swap |\n\n=====================================================================\n7. PAGING ETIQUETTE AND AFTER-ACTIONS (reference)\n=====================================================================\n7.1 Acknowledge within 10 minutes; open an incident channel for SEV1/SEV2.\n7.2 For SEV2 on a tier-1 service, post a status update within 30 minutes even if customer impact is unclear.\n7.3 Hand-over notes are written in the rotation's wiki page at each hand-over.\n7.4 After an override ends, the scheduled primary resumes automatically; no manual hand-over is needed.\n7.5 Night pages (22:00\u201307:00 local) earn a compensation credit; log it in the HR tool within 7 days.\n7.6 If the paging tool is down, call the phone number in the rotation's wiki page.\n\n=====================================================================\n8. ALERT TO ROUTE\n=====================================================================\nAlertmanager, 2026-09-21T06:30:12Z\n  rule: ProcessCrashLoop\n  labels: process=\"ledgerd\", host=\"pay-eu-7\", env=\"prod\", restarts_15m=\"9\"\n  summary: \"ledgerd restarting repeatedly (OOMKilled)\"\nChat, #sre: \"ledgerd... that's ledger-reports, right? Data-Finance, so just a ticket.\" \u2014 \"No wait, Payments-Core. Dmitri is on their sheet this week.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.7807818651199341, "correct": false, "expected": "2", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-multi_hop-05", "item_sha256": "1f6263258ac055622974127ce8829f1bee02f036ce846b0c99ad056b31647fe1", "latency_ms": 393.28304305672646, "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3", "4"], "prediction": "1", "probabilities": {"0": 0.026716886088252068, "1": 0.7807818651199341, "2": 0.10566733777523041, "3": 0.05655965209007263, "4": 0.030274197459220886}, "source_split": "public:multi_hop", "state": "KESTREL VALLEY COMMUNITY COLLEGE \u2014 HARDSHIP BURSARY REGULATIONS 2026/27\n(Approved by the Student Finance Committee, 2026-05-12. Applies to awards for the academic year starting 2026-09-01.)\n\nPART A \u2014 AWARD LEVELS\nA.1 The bursary is paid at one of five levels:\n    Level 0 \u2014 no award\n    Level 1 \u2014 EUR 500\n    Level 2 \u2014 EUR 1,200\n    Level 3 \u2014 EUR 2,000\n    Level 4 \u2014 EUR 3,000\nA.2 The level is determined by the PER-CAPITA COUNTABLE INCOME of the assessed household (Part D), using the table in Part E, subject to the asset rule in Part F.\n\nPART B \u2014 DEPENDENCY STATUS\nB.1 A student is INDEPENDENT if on the reference date (1 September of the award year) at least one of the following is true:\n    (a) the student is 24 years old or older;\n    (b) the student is married;\n    (c) the student has at least one dependent child living with them;\n    (d) the student was in local-authority care after their 16th birthday.\nB.2 Otherwise the student is DEPENDENT.\nB.3 For all purposes of these regulations, a registered civil partnership is treated exactly like a marriage, and a registered civil partner exactly like a spouse.\nB.4 Dependency status is fixed on the reference date; later changes apply only from the next award year.\n\nPART C \u2014 ASSESSED HOUSEHOLD\nC.1 Dependent students: the assessed household is the parent(s) the student normally lives with, their spouse/partner if any, the student, and all other children living in that household.\nC.2 Independent students: the assessed household is the student, their spouse (if any), and their dependent children (if any). Parents' income and household are NOT assessed for independent students, even if parental data was supplied on the form.\nC.3 Household size = number of persons in the assessed household.\n\nPART D \u2014 COUNTABLE INCOME\nD.1 Countable income = sum over the assessed household (excluding children under 18) of: gross employment income for the previous calendar year (2025), net self-employment profit, pensions, and taxable benefits.\nD.2 Excluded from countable income: child benefit; disability-related allowances; student loans and grants; earnings from the college's WORK-STUDY programme (for any student); one-off gifts under EUR 1,000.\nD.3 Dependent students only: deduct EUR 2,000 from the household's countable income for each OTHER child of the household enrolled full-time in higher education.\nD.4 Per-capita countable income = countable income \u00f7 household size, rounded to the nearest euro.\n\nPART E \u2014 LEVEL TABLE (per-capita countable income, EUR)\n| Per-capita countable income | Level |\n|-----------------------------|-------|\n| up to 7,000                 | 4     |\n| 7,001 \u2013 10,500              | 3     |\n| 10,501 \u2013 14,000             | 2     |\n| 14,001 \u2013 18,000             | 1     |\n| above 18,000                | 0     |\n\nPART F \u2014 ASSETS\nF.1 If the assessed household's savings and investments (excluding the main home and one car) exceed EUR 40,000, the level is capped at Level 1.\nF.2 Assets are declared on the form; the college may request statements.\n\nPART G \u2014 PROCEDURE (reference)\nG.1 Applications close 2026-10-15. Late applications are assessed only if funds remain.\nG.2 Evidence: P60/annual payslip or tax assessment for each income line; certificate for civil status; enrolment certificates for siblings.\nG.3 Payments are made in two instalments (November, February) to the student's own account.\nG.4 Students must be enrolled at least half-time on the census date (2026-10-01).\nG.5 Appeals go to the Student Finance Committee within 28 days of the decision letter.\nG.6 Assessors record the dependency decision, the household composition, each countable and excluded income line, and the per-capita figure on the assessment sheet, so that the decision can be checked by a second assessor.\nG.7 Amounts are taken in whole euros as declared and evidenced; the college does not index 2025 income to 2026 prices.\n\nPART H \u2014 FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS\nQ: I filled in my parents' details because the form asked for them. Does that matter? \u2014 A: The form collects parental data from everyone. Whether it is used depends only on Part B.\nQ: My partner and I are not married but have lived together for years. \u2014 A: Cohabitation without marriage or registered civil partnership does not make a student independent.\nQ: My work-study job is my main income. \u2014 A: Work-study earnings are still excluded (D.2).\nQ: My sibling is at university; can I deduct that? \u2014 A: Only dependent students' households get the D.3 deduction.\n\nPART I \u2014 WORKED EXAMPLES (illustrative, fictional students)\nI.1 Dependent student, age 19, lives with mother (employment 2025: EUR 31,000) and brother (17). Household 3. Countable 31,000. Per capita 10,333 \u2192 Level 3.\nI.2 Dependent student, age 21, lives with both parents (employment EUR 48,000 and EUR 12,000) and a sister (22) studying full-time at university. Household 4. Countable 60,000 \u2212 2,000 (D.3) = 58,000. Per capita 14,500 \u2192 Level 1.\nI.3 Independent student, age 26, single, no children. Employment EUR 11,400; work-study EUR 2,900 (excluded). Household 1. Per capita 11,400 \u2192 Level 2.\nI.4 Independent student, age 22, married, one child aged 2. Spouse employment EUR 24,600; student no income; child benefit EUR 3,000 (excluded). Household 3. Per capita 8,200 \u2192 Level 3.\nI.5 Independent student, age 30, savings EUR 52,000, income EUR 6,000. Per capita 6,000 \u2192 Level 4 by table, capped to Level 1 by F.1.\n\nPART J \u2014 RELATED FUNDS (not assessed on this form)\nJ.1 Childcare Support Fund: separate application; up to EUR 1,500 per year towards registered childcare.\nJ.2 Travel Grant: students living more than 40 km from campus may claim 50 % of a public-transport season ticket.\nJ.3 Emergency Loan: interest-free, up to EUR 800, repayable within the academic year.\nJ.4 Receiving a hardship bursary does not affect eligibility for J.1\u2013J.3.\n\nPART K \u2014 DATA PROTECTION\nK.1 Information supplied is used only to assess student-finance applications and is deleted 6 years after the last payment.\nK.2 Parental information supplied for independent students is deleted at the end of the assessment and is not used.\nK.3 Applicants may request a copy of their data at any time from the Student Finance Office.\n\nPART L \u2014 CHANGE LOG\n2026-05-12  Part E thresholds raised by approx. 5 % for inflation. D.2 extended: work-study earnings now excluded for ALL students (previously dependent students only).\n2025-05-20  B.3 added (registered civil partnerships).\n2024-06-01  F.1 asset cap raised from EUR 30,000 to EUR 40,000.\n\n=====================================================================\nAPPLICATION HB-26-0417 \u2014 received 2026-09-10\n=====================================================================\nSection 1 \u2014 Student\n  Name: Mara Voigt       Student no. KV-2291807      Programme: AS Laboratory Technology, full-time\n  Date of birth: 2002-10-15\n  Civil status: registered civil partnership (certificate dated 2026-06-20, partner: Lea Brandt)\n  Dependent children: none\n  Local-authority care history: none\n  Address: M\u00fchlweg 3, 34131 Kassel (shared with partner)\n\nSection 2 \u2014 Student's and partner's income, calendar year 2025\n  | Person      | Source                                        | Amount EUR |\n  |-------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------:|\n  | Mara Voigt  | Part-time job, Apotheke am Markt (employment) |      7,200 |\n  | Mara Voigt  | KVCC work-study (library assistant)           |      3,600 |\n  | Mara Voigt  | Birthday gift from grandmother                |        500 |\n  | Lea Brandt  | Employment, Stadtwerke Kassel (gross)         |     20,600 |\n  Evidence attached and checked by the office: annual payslips for both employments, KVCC work-study statement, civil-partnership certificate.\n\nSection 3 \u2014 Parents' household (completed by applicant)\n  Father: Jens Voigt, employment income 2025: EUR 64,000\n  Mother: Sabine Voigt, employment income 2025: EUR 22,000; child benefit received: EUR 3,000\n  Other children in parents' household: Tim Voigt (20, full-time university student), Lena Voigt (15)\n  Parents' address: Am Hang 12, 34270 Schauenburg\n\nSection 4 \u2014 Assets\n  Mara and Lea joint savings account: EUR 9,400. No investments. Car: one (2014 hatchback).\n\nAssessor's first note (trainee): \"Mara is 23 on 1 Sept \u2192 dependent. Use parents: 64,000 + 22,000 \u2212 2,000 (Tim) = 84,000 over 5 persons = 16,800 \u2192 Level 1.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.36593005061149597, "correct": false, "expected": "credit_20_percent", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-multi_hop-07", "item_sha256": "838ac79d0444e7810fe933e442f0e880ac71f3bda24d736e109bb82816c9fc35", "latency_ms": 454.0094193071127, "options": ["no_credit", "credit_10_percent", "credit_20_percent", "credit_25_percent", "credit_50_percent", "credit_full_month"], "prediction": "credit_25_percent", "probabilities": {"credit_10_percent": 0.1525423377752304, "credit_20_percent": 0.28498661518096924, "credit_25_percent": 0.36593005061149597, "credit_50_percent": 0.118800088763237, "credit_full_month": 0.0340367928147316, "no_credit": 0.043704111129045486}, "source_split": "public:multi_hop", "state": "WESERLINK TELECOM \u2014 HOME AIR 5G (fixed wireless) OUTAGE CREDIT PROCEDURE, customer care guideline CG-31, version 2026-08\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 1 \u2014 RULES\n=====================================================================\n1.1 Credits are calculated per BILLING PERIOD. A customer's billing period runs from their billing day (inclusive) to the day before the next billing day. Only outage time inside the billing period counts; an outage crossing a boundary counts only for the part inside.\n1.2 Identify the radio cell serving the customer's service address using the address-to-cell register (Part 3). Outages are logged per cell (Part 4).\n1.3 Cell renaming: during the 2026 network migration, cells were renamed. The legacy network management system (NMS-L) keeps logging the OLD cell name until the site is migrated to NMS-2. Treat old and new names from the rename table (Part 3b) as the same cell.\n1.4 Counted outages: only log entries with impact \"SERVICE-AFFECTING\" count. Entries with impact \"DEGRADED\" (slower speeds, service still available) do not count.\n1.5 Planned maintenance does not count if it was announced to customers at least 48 hours before it started. Planned maintenance announced later than that counts like an unplanned outage.\n1.6 Add up all counted outage time for the serving cell in the billing period (hours and minutes), then apply the credit table (Part 2).\n1.7 Business plans: for plans classified as BUSINESS in the plan catalogue (Part 5), the credit percentage from Part 2 is doubled (maximum 100 %).\n1.8 Credits are applied automatically to the next invoice; agents select the credit label in the CRM.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 2 \u2014 CREDIT TABLE (cumulative counted outage time in the billing period)\n=====================================================================\n| Counted outage time            | Credit (% of monthly plan fee) |\n|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| less than 12 h                 | 0 %                            |\n| 12 h to less than 24 h         | 10 %                           |\n| 24 h to less than 48 h         | 25 %                           |\n| 48 h to less than 72 h         | 50 %                           |\n| 72 h or more                   | 100 % (full month)             |\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 3 \u2014 ADDRESS-TO-CELL REGISTER (Bremen, extract)\n=====================================================================\n| Service address                     | Postcode | Serving cell    | Fallback cell   |\n|-------------------------------------|----------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| Am Wall 110                         | 28195    | HB-MITTE-0411   | HB-MITTE-0412   |\n| B\u00fcrgermeister-Smidt-Str. 20         | 28195    | HB-MITTE-0413   | HB-MITTE-0411   |\n| Lindenallee 4                       | 28195    | HB-MITTE-0412   | HB-MITTE-0413   |\n| Lindenallee 17                      | 28195    | HB-MITTE-0413   | HB-MITTE-0412   |\n| Ostertorsteinweg 58                 | 28203    | HB-OST-0107     | HB-OST-0108     |\n| Schwachhauser Heerstr. 190          | 28211    | HB-SCHW-0220    | HB-SCHW-0221    |\nFallback cells do not count for credits: only the serving cell's outages count, even if the customer's router roamed to the fallback cell.\n\nPART 3b \u2014 CELL RENAME TABLE (network migration 2026)\n| Old name (NMS-L) | New name (NMS-2) | Renamed on  | Site migrated to NMS-2 |\n|------------------|------------------|-------------|------------------------|\n| HB-C-110         | HB-MITTE-0411    | 2026-08-15  | 2026-08-20             |\n| HB-C-112         | HB-MITTE-0412    | 2026-08-15  | not yet                |\n| HB-C-113         | HB-MITTE-0413    | 2026-08-15  | 2026-09-02             |\n| HB-E-07          | HB-OST-0107      | 2026-07-01  | 2026-07-01             |\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 4 \u2014 OUTAGE LOG (Bremen Mitte cells, 2026-08-01 to 2026-09-19; times CEST)\n=====================================================================\n| Incident | Cell          | Impact            | Start            | End (restored)   | Type        | Customer announcement |\n|----------|---------------|-------------------|------------------|------------------|-------------|-----------------------|\n| INC-3301 | HB-C-112      | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-08-12 06:00 | 2026-08-12 12:00 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| INC-3340 | HB-C-110      | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-08-19 13:00 | 2026-08-19 20:30 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| INC-3388 | HB-C-112      | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-08-22 09:10 | 2026-08-22 17:40 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| INC-3402 | HB-MITTE-0413 | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-08-30 02:00 | 2026-08-31 06:00 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| INC-3415 | HB-MITTE-0412 | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-09-03 22:00 | 2026-09-04 07:15 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| MNT-0091 | HB-MITTE-0412 | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-09-10 01:00 | 2026-09-10 08:00 | planned     | SMS + e-mail sent 2026-09-06 10:00 |\n| INC-3431 | HB-C-112      | DEGRADED          | 2026-09-12 08:00 | 2026-09-13 04:00 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| INC-3440 | HB-MITTE-0411 | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-09-15 18:00 | 2026-09-15 23:00 | unplanned   | \u2014                     |\n| MNT-0097 | HB-MITTE-0413 | SERVICE-AFFECTING | 2026-09-17 00:30 | 2026-09-17 03:30 | planned     | SMS sent 2026-09-16 12:00 |\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 5 \u2014 PLAN CATALOGUE (Home Air 5G family)\n=====================================================================\n| Plan code  | Marketing name                     | Monthly fee | Classification |\n|------------|------------------------------------|------------:|----------------|\n| HA5G-S     | Home Air 5G S                      |  EUR 29.99  | CONSUMER       |\n| HA5G-M     | Home Air 5G M                      |  EUR 39.99  | CONSUMER       |\n| HA5G-M-SB  | Home Air 5G M (Small Business)     |  EUR 44.99  | BUSINESS       |\n| HA5G-L     | Home Air 5G L                      |  EUR 49.99  | CONSUMER       |\n| HA5G-L-SB  | Home Air 5G L (Small Business)     |  EUR 54.99  | BUSINESS       |\nNote: Small Business variants are sold through the consumer web shop and appear under the same product family on invoices (\"Home Air 5G M\").\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 6 \u2014 AGENT NOTES (reference)\n=====================================================================\n6.1 Do not promise credits on the phone before running this procedure.\n6.2 Goodwill vouchers (max EUR 10) are separate from outage credits and need a team lead.\n6.3 Customers who report outages that do not appear in the log: open a network ticket; no credit until the log is updated.\n6.4 A customer's own speed tests are not evidence of an outage.\n6.5 For outages longer than 72 h, also offer a free mobile data pass (separate process).\n6.6 Record in the CRM which log entries were counted and which were excluded, with the reason (outside period, degraded, announced maintenance, other cell).\n6.7 If the customer disputes the result, escalate to the Network Credits desk; do not recalculate by hand a second time.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 7 \u2014 CASE\n=====================================================================\nCustomer account WL-4471902, \"Fahrradwerkstatt Kuhn\" (contact: B. Kuhn)\nService address: Lindenallee 4, 28195 Bremen\nPlan code: HA5G-M-SB     Billing day: 20th of each month\nContact 2026-09-19: \"My internet was down several times since the last invoice \u2014 once for a whole night in early September and again that Thursday in August, plus the maintenance night. And last weekend it was crawling for almost a day. What do I get?\"\nPrevious agent note: \"Checked HB-MITTE-0412 in the log: 9 h 15 min unplanned + 7 h maintenance. Consumer plan (invoice says Home Air 5G M). \u2192 10 %.\"\n"}
{"confidence": 0.9324532747268677, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-multi_hop-08", "item_sha256": "5cf41e630f21e3d9bbbdc8cc7db5ecdb88177df190828eed85fd4783d95062ae", "latency_ms": 407.79896453022957, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.06754669547080994, "yes": 0.9324532747268677}, "source_split": "public:multi_hop", "state": "BRIGHTLINE GROUP \u2014 HR SHARED SERVICES KNOWLEDGE BASE\nArticle HR-KB-212: \"Parental-leave salary top-up (Elterngeld-Aufstockung) \u2014 eligibility check\" (last updated 2026-07-14)\n\n=====================================================================\n1. PROCEDURE\n=====================================================================\nStep 1. Find the employee's PAYROLL COMPANY code in the personnel record (field \"PayCo\").\nStep 2. Map the payroll company to the employing LEGAL ENTITY (table 2). Cost-centre codes are NOT a reliable guide to the legal entity, because cost centres are shared across entities.\nStep 3. Find the collective agreement that covers that legal entity ON THE LEAVE START DATE (table 3).\nStep 4. Apply that agreement's top-up conditions (table 4), including its service-counting footnotes.\nStep 5. The employee is eligible only if every condition of the applicable agreement is met on the leave start date. Conditions of other agreements in the group are irrelevant, even if the employee worked under them earlier.\n\n=====================================================================\n2. PAYROLL COMPANY \u2192 LEGAL ENTITY\n=====================================================================\n| PayCo | Legal entity code | Legal entity name                    | Seat      |\n|-------|-------------------|--------------------------------------|-----------|\n| 0110  | LE-01             | Brightline Group SE                  | Hamburg   |\n| 0220  | LE-02             | Brightline Logistik GmbH             | Bremen    |\n| 0310  | LE-03             | Brightline Retail GmbH               | Hamburg   |\n| 0450  | LE-04             | Brightline Services GmbH             | Hamburg   |\n| 0460  | LE-02             | Brightline Logistik GmbH (Hamburg branch payroll) | Hamburg |\n| 0510  | LE-05             | Brightline IT Solutions GmbH         | Berlin    |\n\n=====================================================================\n3. LEGAL ENTITY \u2192 COLLECTIVE AGREEMENT\n=====================================================================\n| Legal entity | Agreement      | Valid from  | Valid to    |\n|--------------|----------------|-------------|-------------|\n| LE-01        | none (company policy CP-7) | 2019-01-01 |      |\n| LE-02        | CA-LOG-2023    | 2023-04-01  |             |\n| LE-03        | CA-RET-2022    | 2022-01-01  |             |\n| LE-04        | none (company policy CP-7) | 2020-06-01 | 2025-12-31 |\n| LE-04        | CA-SVC-2025    | 2026-01-01  |             |\n| LE-05        | CA-IT-2024     | 2024-07-01  |             |\n\n=====================================================================\n4. TOP-UP CONDITIONS BY AGREEMENT\n=====================================================================\n| Agreement / policy | Top-up amount                        | Minimum continuous service at leave start | Minimum working time        | Other |\n|--------------------|--------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|-------|\n| CP-7               | none                                 | \u2014                                         | \u2014                           | CP-7 has no top-up |\n| CA-LOG-2023        | to 80 % of net pay, max 3 months     | 6 months (fn. 1)                          | \u2265 50 % FTE                  |       |\n| CA-RET-2022        | to 75 % of net pay, max 2 months     | 12 months (fn. 1)                         | \u2265 40 % FTE                  |       |\n| CA-SVC-2025        | to 85 % of net pay, max 4 months     | 12 months (fn. 2)                         | \u2265 50 % FTE (avg. last 3 months) | leave must be notified \u2265 7 weeks before start |\n| CA-IT-2024         | to 90 % of net pay, max 2 months     | 12 months (fn. 2)                         | \u2265 50 % FTE                  |       |\nFootnote 1: Service with the current legal entity only.\nFootnote 2: Service with the current legal entity, PLUS earlier service with any Brightline Group entity if the break between the end of the earlier employment and the start of the current one is no longer than 90 calendar days. If the break is longer, only service with the current entity counts. Transfers under a formal business transfer (table 5) always count as continuous.\n\n=====================================================================\n5. FORMAL BUSINESS TRANSFERS (\u00a7613a BGB-style transfers between entities)\n=====================================================================\n| Date       | From  | To    | Scope                                    |\n|------------|-------|-------|------------------------------------------|\n| 2024-01-01 | LE-03 | LE-04 | Customer service centre Hamburg (41 staff) |\n| 2025-04-01 | LE-02 | LE-05 | Warehouse IT team (6 staff)              |\nIndividual moves between entities (resignation and new hire, or new contract) are NOT business transfers.\n\n=====================================================================\n6. PERSONNEL RECORD\n=====================================================================\nEmployee: Sophie Marquardt     Personnel no.: 88-20417\nPayCo: 0450        Cost centre: BL-LOG-HH-22 (\"Logistics support Hamburg\", shared cost centre)\nCurrent contract: start 2025-11-10, permanent, 32 h/week (0.8 FTE) since start; no changes.\nEmployment history in the group:\n  - 2021-03-01 to 2025-07-31: Brightline Logistik GmbH (PayCo 0460), warehouse planner, 1.0 FTE. Ended by employee resignation (moved abroad).\n  - 2025-11-10 to date: current contract (see above), new hire after application via careers site.\nParental leave: notified 2026-08-03; planned start 2026-10-05; duration 12 months.\nHR generalist comment: \"Sophie has been with Brightline since 2021 and she's in Logistics (see cost centre) \u2014 CA-LOG needs only 6 months. Eligible, top-up to 80 %.\"\n\n=====================================================================\n7. GENERAL NOTES (reference)\n=====================================================================\n7.1 The statutory parental allowance is paid by the state; the top-up is paid by the employer on top of it.\n7.2 Top-up payments are pro-rated for part-time employees based on contractual FTE.\n7.3 Employees on fixed-term contracts are eligible under all agreements if the contract runs beyond the leave start date.\n7.4 The eligibility check must be completed within 10 working days of the leave notification.\n7.5 A negative decision must state the unmet condition and the applicable agreement.\n7.6 Notification periods are counted in calendar weeks from the date HR received the written notification.\n\n=====================================================================\n8. FAQ AND WORKED EXAMPLES\n=====================================================================\nQ: An employee's cost centre belongs to another entity's department. Which agreement applies? \u2014 A: The legal entity from the PayCo mapping, always (step 2).\nQ: Does an apprenticeship with a group entity count as service? \u2014 A: Yes, if it was with the same entity (fn. 1) or within the fn. 2 rules.\nQ: Is the 90-day break in fn. 2 counted in working days? \u2014 A: No, calendar days, from the day after the earlier employment ended to the day before the new one started.\nExample A: Employee of LE-05 since 2025-12-01; before that LE-03 from 2019 to 2025-10-15 (break 46 days); leave starts 2026-06-01. CA-IT-2024 fn. 2 \u2192 LE-03 service counts \u2192 eligible (if FTE \u2265 50 %).\nExample B: Employee of LE-03 since 2026-02-01, previously LE-02 for 8 years until 2026-01-31; leave starts 2026-09-01. CA-RET-2022 fn. 1 \u2192 only LE-03 service (7 months) counts \u2192 not eligible.\nExample C: Employee transferred LE-03 \u2192 LE-04 on 2024-01-01 under the table-5 business transfer; LE-03 service since 2018 counts as continuous under fn. 2.\nExample D: Employee of LE-01 \u2192 CP-7 has no top-up \u2192 not eligible regardless of service.\n\n=====================================================================\n9. CHANGE LOG\n=====================================================================\n2026-07-14  Table 3: CA-SVC-2025 added for LE-04 from 2026-01-01 (previously company policy CP-7). Footnote 2 break limit reduced from 180 to 90 calendar days (all agreements using footnote 2).\n2020-02-01  PayCo 0460 created for the Hamburg branch payroll of LE-02.\n2025-06-30  CA-LOG-2023 minimum service lowered from 12 to 6 months.\n"}
{"confidence": 0.8092672228813171, "correct": true, "expected": "merchant_wins", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-opus-b-probability-01", "item_sha256": "875ba0d885227ed8630e5f46fc5b4dcef1ac501a4a0d937e96a701483e435652", "latency_ms": 306.2362363561988, "options": ["customer_wins", "merchant_wins", "customer_withdraws"], "prediction": "merchant_wins", "probabilities": {"customer_wins": 0.15935415029525757, "customer_withdraws": 0.031378693878650665, "merchant_wins": 0.8092672228813171}, "source_split": "public:probability", "state": "VOLTARIA ONLINE STORE \u2014 CHARGEBACK DESK, dispute forecast worksheet\n\nForecasting rule (desk standard FS-2): the forecast for a new dispute is the distribution of outcomes among CLOSED past disputes that are COMPARABLE to it. Comparable means all three of:\n  (1) same product line as the new dispute;\n  (2) same card-network reason category;\n  (3) tracking status in the same group \u2014 the groups are \"delivered\" (delivered_signed and delivered_unsigned together), \"in_transit\", and \"no_tracking\".\nDisputes with outcome OPEN are not closed and are excluded. Amount and date do not affect comparability.\n\nNEW DISPUTE CB-4190: product line Electronics (noise-cancelling headphones, EUR 289), reason category item_not_received, tracking status delivered_unsigned (left at door, photo on file). Evidence package submitted 2026-09-18.\n\nPAST DISPUTES, Mar\u2013Aug 2026 (complete log)\n| ID      | Opened     | Product line | Reason            | Tracking           | EUR   | Outcome       |\n|---------|------------|-------------|-------------------|--------------------|-------|---------------|\n| CB-4100 | 2026-03-03 | Electronics | item_not_received | in_transit         |  1300 | customer_won  |\n| CB-4101 | 2026-03-28 | Electronics | item_not_received | delivered_unsigned |   492 | merchant_won  |\n| CB-4102 | 2026-04-02 | Electronics | item_not_received | delivered_signed   |   945 | merchant_won  |\n| CB-4103 | 2026-08-28 | El