{"confidence": 0.5926666259765625, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-02", "item_sha256": "64c4804003df655bbf9b1ff169b39daa0ddb0bbb74309a0f422fb041f6671450", "latency_ms": 84.45239439606667, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.5926666259765625, "yes": 0.4073334336280823}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Aster Lane\u2019s refund desk has a rule: a shipment is \u201cunopened\u201d only if every factory seal remains intact. A torn outer courier bag is irrelevant. Customer Len writes, \u201cI never opened the parcel, although the clear seal over the product box split when I lifted it from the wet mailer.\u201d An intake photo shows the courier bag torn, the cardboard product box closed, and its clear factory seal split end to end. The agent\u2019s note says, \u201cCustomer says unopened; likely eligible.\u201d Eligibility for the instant unopened-item refund requires return within 30 days and an unopened product. The return was requested on day 12. A separate defect route may apply after inspection, but the question concerns the instant unopened-item route only.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Inspection protocol P-8 defines \u201cfactory sealed\u201d by every manufacturer-applied seal being continuous at inspection. A reseller wrap and an intact inner moisture strip are evidence of condition but cannot replace a split factory seal. The intake badge saying SEALED is generated from the seller declaration, not inspection."}
{"confidence": 0.9959298968315125, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-04", "item_sha256": "7a6e164e5bfe1b5355a42b8e8bec2009f35b6b8512e1da42779905644576146d", "latency_ms": 78.45271192491055, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.9959298968315125, "yes": 0.004070138093084097}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "QuillWorks evaluates whether a reviewer endorsed a proposal. In the meeting transcript, reviewer Jo says: \u201cSam wrote in the draft chat, \u2018This plan is flawless; ship it now.\u2019 I am quoting that because it captures exactly the overconfidence we must avoid. My own recommendation is not to ship until the load test passes.\u201d Later Jo answers \u201ccorrect\u201d when asked whether Sam had favored immediate shipment. The minutes bot extracts the sentence \u201cThis plan is flawless; ship it now\u201d and attributes it to Jo because Jo spoke the words aloud. The rubric asks about Jo\u2019s own recommendation, not whether Jo accurately reported another person\u2019s position."}
{"confidence": 0.5423436760902405, "correct": false, "expected": "0", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-05", "item_sha256": "22bb2ad20a462a87164aa34bb1a9461aecd80f651eb5f0e9a5b2f00fb914f4cf", "latency_ms": 74.5796225965023, "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "prediction": "2", "probabilities": {"0": 0.19951710104942322, "1": 0.12101323157548904, "2": 0.5423436760902405, "3": 0.13712595403194427}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Morrow Books \u2014 fulfillment-control extract, revision 6. Section 2 assigns exception levels: 0 means no operative exception; 1 means requested but unauthorized; 2 means authorized but unused; 3 means used for a shipment. Section 4 says an authorization may be withdrawn until the warehouse first relies on it by bypassing validation or releasing the parcel. Section 7 says packing notes are evidentiary only: copying an authorization does not itself constitute reliance, and a note copied after withdrawal does not restore authority. A portal address correction processed normally is not use of an exception.\n\nCase 44: On 3 May a supervisor authorized one address override. At 08:15 on 4 May the customer corrected the address through the ordinary portal. At 08:22 the supervisor wrote, \u201cWithdraw the override; standard address validation now applies.\u201d At 08:31 a clerk, working from yesterday's queue, copied the authorization into the packing note and marked \u201coverride ready.\u201d The validation log at 08:44 shows the corrected address passed standard validation; bypass_used=false. The parcel remains in the cage. A shift summary calls the order \u201cauthorized for override,\u201d but it was generated from packing-note text rather than the authority register."}
{"confidence": 0.8980642557144165, "correct": true, "expected": "keep_open_unresolved", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-06", "item_sha256": "d2b24588f7719eecd5b0a47e8fa12c73130bd3accedaab63476ba4973b1ffcf3", "latency_ms": 77.72597577422857, "options": ["close_confirmed", "keep_open_unresolved", "close_agent_verified", "await_internal_metrics"], "prediction": "keep_open_unresolved", "probabilities": {"await_internal_metrics": 0.016448620706796646, "close_agent_verified": 0.050665322691202164, "close_confirmed": 0.034821730107069016, "keep_open_unresolved": 0.8980642557144165}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Bright Orchard support handbook, sections 5\u20138. A ticket may close when the customer reports the affected function working after remediation. An advance statement such as \u201cif X fixes it, close\u201d records conditional consent, not a report that X worked. Agent telemetry can corroborate a customer report but cannot replace one for an interface-visible defect. If reports conflict, evidence about the customer's affected interface outranks backend health; automated summaries never establish customer confirmation.\n\nTicket BO-771 concerns a dashboard that renders blank for the customer. At 10:02 the customer wrote, \u201cIf resetting the gateway fixes it, you can close this.\u201d The agent reset it at 10:14. Gateway telemetry was normal from 10:16 through 11:16, and an automated card displayed RESOLUTION LIKELY. At 11:18 the agent wrote \u201cReset worked, closing as authorized.\u201d At 11:23 the customer replied from the affected account, \u201cThe dashboard is still blank for me. Please do not close.\u201d A synthetic test at 11:25 loaded the dashboard, but no later customer report exists."}
{"confidence": 0.9628337025642395, "correct": true, "expected": "late", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-08", "item_sha256": "a8122b71cb4ed13df4862740ca028234bfbee1c8a9f21c8f042ddaa43f930a6e", "latency_ms": 71.07409182935953, "options": ["on_time", "late", "indeterminate", "canceled"], "prediction": "late", "probabilities": {"canceled": 0.00028504186775535345, "indeterminate": 0.003934881649911404, "late": 0.9628337025642395, "on_time": 0.03294635936617851}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Vela Transit awards the \u201con-time\u201d status when arrival is no later than 18:00 at the destination clock. Dispatch predicted 17:52. A monitoring banner at 17:55 read \u201cON TIME,\u201d but banners are forecasts and freeze once a trip enters the tunnel. The actual gate scan, which is the authoritative arrival record, occurred at 18:04. A passenger message says the bus reached the station \u201caround six,\u201d and the driver\u2019s handwritten log says 17:59; policy resolves discrepancies in favor of the gate scan unless it is flagged faulty. No fault flag exists.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Timing standard T4 uses the first unflagged physical gate scan. Manual estimates, scans marked TEST, and dispatch-board projections do not substitute; equality with the deadline is on time."}
{"confidence": 0.5879875421524048, "correct": true, "expected": "blocked_second_review", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-10", "item_sha256": "16325dfe6851256deae6e0a03f2bf41be6b1917910b51f769e89693c422d0106", "latency_ms": 74.70441050827503, "options": ["ready_one_review", "ready_two_reviews", "blocked_second_review", "blocked_failed_test"], "prediction": "blocked_second_review", "probabilities": {"blocked_failed_test": 0.07957546412944794, "blocked_second_review": 0.5879875421524048, "ready_one_review": 0.05469135940074921, "ready_two_reviews": 0.27774566411972046}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Granite Finch requires two independent reviewers unless the change is documentation-only. The release note calls patch P-7 \u201cdocumentation cleanup,\u201d but the diff summary says it changes a timeout constant from 30 to 45 seconds and fixes three comments. Classification follows changed behavior, not the release-note title. Reviewer A approved. Reviewer B wrote, \u201cI would approve after the timeout test passes.\u201d The test passed later, but Reviewer B never sent a new approval; conditional intentions are not approvals."}
{"confidence": 0.9940890073776245, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-13", "item_sha256": "6cf207cb1d5bc98c8d64aff43cbb4e28d1ccdbc07032298a1b6be7d48e092fca", "latency_ms": 74.45763982832432, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.9940890073776245, "yes": 0.005911068990826607}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Lumen Press asks whether an author requested deletion of chapter 6. The author's message says, \u201cMy editor asked, \u2018Should we delete chapter 6?\u2019 Please do not take that quoted question as my instruction. I want chapter 6 retained, but delete the duplicate appendix.\u201d An extraction system lists \u201cdelete chapter 6\u201d as a detected imperative after dropping punctuation and attribution. The decision must follow the author's communicative intent in context.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Editorial deletion requires an affirmative instruction identifying the material to remove. Discussion of a possible cut, quoted advice, and an instruction to retain the chapter are interpreted by the latest direct author instruction."}
{"confidence": 0.7310585379600525, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-a-trap-15", "item_sha256": "e37233837381acd83fcbc3d59e0dd47b7290cc3b056de190d0ee9904cec8516d", "latency_ms": 79.32290155440569, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.7310585379600525, "yes": 0.2689414322376251}, "source_split": "public:trap", "state": "Nacre School attendance policy, revision 2026-2. A guardian notice excuses an absence if it is operative before the scheduled class start. A guardian may withdraw a notice before that start; a withdrawn notice is then treated as absent. Reinstatement is a new notice and uses its own receipt time. Messages sent after the scheduled start cannot satisfy the advance-notice route, although the separate emergency-review route may be used when an emergency is documented. Office acknowledgements record receipt only and do not prevent withdrawal.\n\nFor Toma's 09:00 class, a guardian submitted \u201cToma will be absent; please excuse\u201d at 07:30. The office acknowledged it at 07:42. At 08:10 the same authenticated guardian wrote, \u201cPlease disregard my excuse; Toma will attend.\u201d Toma did not attend. At 09:20 the guardian wrote, \u201cI should not have withdrawn it; please use the first message.\u201d No emergency or documentation is claimed. The attendance screen still shows the 07:30 message first because it sorts by conversation start."}
{"confidence": 0.9914224743843079, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-01", "item_sha256": "12df28745cead88b7212c5f4e6f0c3b1cd64700e9c47047f525d6926c20d2a7d", "latency_ms": 80.7478316128254, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.008577484637498856, "yes": 0.9914224743843079}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "A theater sold 240 tickets. Adult tickets cost $18 and student tickets cost $11. Total receipts were $3,508. Determine the number of each ticket type, and show an equation check using both the count and revenue totals. Do not merely state the result.", "response": "Let a be adult tickets and s be student tickets. Then a+s=240 and 18a+11s=3508. Subtracting 11(a+s)=2640 from the revenue equation gives 7a=868, so a=124 and s=116. Checks: 124+116=240; 18(124)+11(116)=2232+1276=3508. Thus 124 adult and 116 student tickets were sold."}}
{"confidence": 0.9820137619972229, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-02", "item_sha256": "14e508cf5c1ec2b540fc2f66ea31973e6b7bff78de96048bfdae288e7c80016a", "latency_ms": 72.03029654920101, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.01798621006309986, "yes": 0.9820137619972229}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "A tank starts with 85 liters. It leaks 1.8 liters per hour for 7.5 hours, then 12 liters are added. Report the final volume in liters to one decimal place and show the calculation in chronological order.", "response": "The leak removes 1.8 \u00d7 7.5 = 13.5 liters. After the leak the tank has 85 \u2212 13.5 = 71.5 liters. Adding 12 liters gives 84.5 liters. Final volume: 84.5 L."}}
{"confidence": 0.970687747001648, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-05", "item_sha256": "530ea56f89d2ecb19daecc0ad12ed8ba1514bb0f6032edd5444279e5f2754d8a", "latency_ms": 82.264244556427, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.02931223064661026, "yes": 0.970687747001648}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "Implement Python function chunks(xs, n) returning consecutive lists of at most n items. Requirements: raise ValueError when n <= 0; accept any iterable, including a one-shot generator; an empty iterable returns an empty list.", "response": "```python\ndef chunks(xs, n):\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError('n must be positive')\n    result = []\n    current = []\n    for item in xs:\n        current.append(item)\n        if len(current) == n:\n            result.append(current)\n            current = []\n    if current:\n        result.append(current)\n    return result\n```"}}
{"confidence": 0.9840936064720154, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-08", "item_sha256": "f814ef6dcd0f2e7fe92fe766a858111336c8028d8b4fc4b5e7423d4d4503d61f", "latency_ms": 74.74869210273027, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.01590639166533947, "yes": 0.9840936064720154}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "Convert 250 square feet to square meters using exactly 1 foot = 0.3048 meter. Show how the squared conversion factor is applied and give the answer to three decimal places.", "response": "Because the measurement is area, use the factor twice: 250 ft\u00b2 \u00d7 (0.3048 m/ft)\u00b2 = 250 \u00d7 0.09290304 m\u00b2 = 23.22576 m\u00b2. To three decimal places: 23.225 m\u00b2."}}
