{"confidence": 0.35339924693107605, "correct": false, "expected": "reject_entire_claim", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-01", "item_sha256": "50923c3eea7f0f98cae33fba7ce56e0f7a5ebb0a9d1771bbafdfd8d8f681a30f", "latency_ms": 423.1071174144745, "options": ["approve_all", "approve_meals_only", "approve_lodging_only", "reject_entire_claim"], "prediction": "approve_all", "probabilities": {"approve_all": 0.35339924693107605, "approve_lodging_only": 0.35339924693107605, "approve_meals_only": 0.0788540318608284, "reject_entire_claim": 0.2143474817276001}, "source_split": "public:long_policy", "state": "TRAVEL REIMBURSEMENT MANUAL \u2014 revision 7\n1. Purpose and order of authority. This manual governs employee travel reimbursements submitted from 1 April 2026. Defined terms in section 2 apply everywhere, including appendices. If a case-specific written pre-approval conflicts with the standard limits, the pre-approval controls only where section 6 permits it. Amendment A at the end was adopted later than all numbered sections and controls any inconsistency.\n\n2. Definitions. A \"travel day\" is a calendar day on which the employee is away from the home work location at midnight, except the departure day is also a travel day even when departure is after midnight local time due to a delayed carrier. A \"provided meal\" is food included without separately stated cost in lodging, conference registration, or carrier service; dietary unsuitability does not stop it being provided unless the accessibility office documented the restriction before travel. \"Remote-site travel\" means the destination is more than 80 road kilometers from both the employee's home and assigned office. \"Timely\" means received in the expense portal within 20 business days after the final travel day. Business days exclude weekends and the company closure dates listed in section 9.\n\n3. Meal allowance. The standard allowance is 68 credits per full travel day. Departure and return days receive 75 percent. Deduct 20 percent for a provided breakfast, 30 percent for lunch, and 50 percent for dinner; percentages are of the unadjusted 68-credit daily allowance. The daily result cannot be below zero. Receipts neither increase nor decrease this allowance.\n\n4. Lodging. Lodging is reimbursable up to 190 credits per night. Taxes stated separately are outside that cap. A conference hotel may be reimbursed up to 240 credits only when the booking was made before the conference block closed and the conference organizer named that hotel. Personal upgrades are never reimbursable.\n\n5. Ground transport. Rail is preferred. A taxi over 55 credits requires either a transit-disruption record or remote-site travel. A manager's convenience statement is not a disruption record.\n\n6. Pre-approval. A director may pre-approve lodging above the standard cap or taxi use without a disruption. The writing must identify the trip and a maximum amount. Pre-approval cannot waive timeliness, turn a personal upgrade into business expense, alter meal deductions, or override a later amendment.\n\n7. Late claims. Finance must reject an untimely claim unless the portal was unavailable for at least four continuous hours on the submission deadline and the employee submitted by noon on the next business day. Manager absence, missing receipts, workload, or an email sent outside the portal does not extend the period.\n\n8. Case file. Mira traveled to Quarry Point, 112 road kilometers from her home but 34 road kilometers from her assigned office. She departed Monday 6 April 2026, stayed away Monday and Tuesday at midnight, and returned Wednesday 8 April. Her director pre-approved \"up to 230 credits nightly at the Summit Conference Hotel and taxis up to 90 credits for the Quarry Point trip.\" The organizer named Summit and the block was still open when she booked at 225 credits nightly. Tuesday conference registration included breakfast. The accessibility office had documented before travel that the provided breakfast could not meet her medically required diet. A Tuesday taxi cost 82 credits; transit operated normally. Mira entered the complete claim in the portal on Monday 11 May. The portal was available throughout. Company closures in this interval were Friday 10 April and Friday 1 May.\n\n9. Calendar note. The weekend days and the two stated closure Fridays are not business days. All other Mondays through Fridays in the interval are business days.\n\nAMENDMENT A \u2014 adopted 15 March 2026, effective for travel beginning on or after 1 April 2026. Despite section 6, director pre-approval cannot raise the lodging cap at a named conference hotel while its conference block remains open. Such lodging is capped at the ordinary 190 credits plus separately stated taxes. The amendment does not change taxi authority or the definition of remote-site travel.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND REFERENCE MATERIAL (part of the document):\n\nAppendix 1. receipt imaging. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated as the decision maker unless a controlling section expressly says otherwise. Staff should preserve ordinary supporting material and use the defined business calendar. This appendix supplies administration only: it does not enlarge eligibility, erase a deadline, reverse a precedence rule, or create an exception to a requirement stated in the operative sections. Where examples use names or dates, they illustrate recordkeeping and do not amend the operative case. Historical versions remain searchable for audit purposes, but a superseded version has no prospective effect. Questions are logged with enough context for a reviewer to reproduce the decision without relying on memory. The records team checks completeness monthly and reports aggregate defects; it does not re-decide the underlying case. Reference code TRA-01 is an index locator, not a priority ranking or outcome code.\n\nAppendix 2. currency tables. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated as the decision maker unless a controlling section expressly says otherwise. Staff should preserve ordinary supporting material and use the defined business calendar. This appendix supplies administration only: it does not enlarge eligibility, erase a deadline, reverse a precedence rule, or create an exception to a requirement stated in the operative sections. Where examples use names or dates, they illustrate recordkeeping and do not amend the operative case. Historical versions remain searchable for audit purposes, but a superseded version has no prospective effect. Questions are logged with enough context for a reviewer to reproduce the decision without relying on memory. The records team checks completeness monthly and reports aggregate defects; it does not re-decide the underlying case. Reference code TRA-02 is an index locator, not a priority ranking or outcome code.\n\nAppendix 3. rail reservations. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated as the decision maker unless a controlling section expressly says otherwise. Staff should preserve ordinary supporting material and use the defined business calendar. This appendix supplies administration only: it does not enlarge eligibility, erase a deadline, reverse a precedence rule, or create an exception to a requirement stated in the operative sections. Where examples use names or dates