{"confidence": 0.36166447401046753, "correct": false, "expected": "pass", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-b-temporal_numeric-04", "item_sha256": "27543ef205f0192a9e07255db9909959425618ce070b4873fa60ee3569055003", "latency_ms": 78.13501078635454, "options": ["pass", "narrow_fail", "clear_fail"], "prediction": "narrow_fail", "probabilities": {"clear_fail": 0.3191677927970886, "narrow_fail": 0.36166447401046753, "pass": 0.3191677927970886}, "source_split": "public:temporal_numeric", "state": {"event": "Classify the adjusted result. Bands: pass is at most 0.080%; narrow fail is greater than 0.080% and at most 0.085%; clear fail is greater than 0.085%.", "rules": "A chemical batch may contain at most 0.080 percent impurity by mass, and a reading exactly at the limit passes. A 2.50 kg sample contains 1.65 grams of impurity. Measurement uncertainty is handled conservatively by adding 0.20 gram to impurity mass before comparing. Use the original total sample mass of 2.50 kg for the percentage; do not add the uncertainty amount to total mass."}}
{"confidence": 0.7981867790222168, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-03", "item_sha256": "720f7079d044af5f39c76bcb5ffc09dbc55abb43cccba8903c7dc2015ed22bcc", "latency_ms": 80.27343638241291, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.2018132209777832, "yes": 0.7981867790222168}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "In PostgreSQL, list every customer exactly once with the count of that customer's paid invoices dated in 2025, including customers with zero. Tables are customers(id,name) and invoices(id,customer_id,status,issued_at). Provide one SQL query; do not use a correlated subquery.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The report feeds a customer-retention dashboard, so absence of invoices is meaningful and must appear as a numerical zero. Assume customer IDs are unique, invoice IDs are non-null, timestamps are stored consistently, and multiple qualifying invoices may exist. The query should remain valid when the invoices table has no matching row at all. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "```sql\nSELECT c.id, c.name, COUNT(i.id) AS paid_invoice_count\nFROM customers AS c\nLEFT JOIN invoices AS i ON i.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE i.status = 'paid'\n  AND i.issued_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND i.issued_at <  DATE '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY c.id, c.name\nORDER BY c.id;\n```\n`COUNT(i.id)` avoids counting the NULL-extended row produced by the outer join."}}
{"confidence": 0.9046505093574524, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-05", "item_sha256": "8b91fe8a72bc4238716e98069aa025af103c138b359731b39040671a98629282", "latency_ms": 83.22222530841827, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.09534946084022522, "yes": 0.9046505093574524}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "A Berlin office schedules a call for 09:15 local time on 27 October 2024. Convert it to UTC. State whether daylight saving time is in effect at that instant and give the applicable UTC offset. Europe/Berlin follows the EU transition, with clocks moving from 03:00 CEST to 02:00 CET that morning.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The meeting record is used by colleagues outside Europe, so an unexplained local timestamp is not acceptable. Use the transition fact supplied here rather than assuming that the entire date has one offset. There is no ambiguity from the repeated early-morning hour because the stated appointment occurs well after that interval. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "The rollback has already occurred by 09:15 local time, so daylight saving time is no longer in effect. Europe/Berlin is then CET, UTC+01:00. Subtracting one hour, the call is at **08:15 UTC on 27 October 2024**."}}
{"confidence": 0.9241418242454529, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-07", "item_sha256": "a4d3b4043743cf21fcf4d3d91d41828bff326c67b34ed276d0b3ccf2c49e60c2", "latency_ms": 78.22895795106888, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.07585817575454712, "yes": 0.9241418242454529}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "A lab sample has mass 2.40 kg and volume 1,500 cm\u00b3. Calculate density in g/cm\u00b3 and kg/m\u00b3, show the conversion, and give both answers to three significant figures.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: These values come from a calibration certificate and should not be reinterpreted as litres or pounds. Preserve the given significant precision in the reported values. The two requested densities must describe the same physical quantity, so the conversion between the unit systems should be visible and internally consistent. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "2.40 kg = 2,400 g. Therefore density = 2,400 g / 1,500 cm\u00b3 = **1.60 g/cm\u00b3**. Since 1 g/cm\u00b3 equals 100 kg/m\u00b3, this is **160 kg/m\u00b3** (three significant figures)."}}
{"confidence": 0.7310585379600525, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-08", "item_sha256": "dbc9e42d138555d029c8703c15f000d8a7021b362ac16ea4abc2edc340bc1809", "latency_ms": 79.50906362384558, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.2689414322376251, "yes": 0.7310585379600525}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "For x = 2.675, explain why binary floating point may not produce the desired financial rounding, then use Python Decimal constructed from a string to round to two decimal places with ROUND_HALF_UP. Output exactly two lines: the code expression on line one and `Result: 2.68` on line two. No Markdown fences.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: This is a formatting-sensitive answer consumed by a two-line checker. Imports are assumed to exist and therefore must not be printed as an extra line. The explanation can be concise, but it is still an explicit deliverable; reconcile that requirement with the exact two-line output by incorporating it without adding a third output line. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "Decimal('2.675').quantize(Decimal('0.01'), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)\nResult: 2.68"}}
{"confidence": 0.9626730680465698, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-09", "item_sha256": "b1509a0b572e448e2f58a36c7c14abcfd473273456608fbd0beeae651165ec53", "latency_ms": 69.80165094137192, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.037326883524656296, "yes": 0.9626730680465698}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "Derive the sum of integers from 37 through 83 inclusive without enumerating them. Show the term count and an arithmetic-series calculation, then verify using a different algebraic identity.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The interval is closed at both ends, and negative-number edge cases are irrelevant for these positive bounds. The first method must use the arithmetic-series average and count, while the verification must not merely repeat the same multiplication in different notation. State enough arithmetic that a reviewer can locate an endpoint error. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "There are 83 \u2212 37 + 1 = 47 terms. Their average is (37 + 83)/2 = 60, so the sum is 47 \u00d7 60 = **2,820**. Independently, triangular sums give [83\u00d784/2] \u2212 [36\u00d737/2] = 3,486 \u2212 666 = **2,820**."}}
{"confidence": 0.9820137619972229, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-10", "item_sha256": "e865de5c867b4d195563611ba3c244c97a85fd0c20f21e46b4824798413f2aef", "latency_ms": 73.77031352370977, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.01798621006309986, "yes": 0.9820137619972229}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "Find the smallest integer n such that 1+2+...+n is at least 10,000. Show the quadratic inequality, solve it, and verify both n and n\u22121 numerically.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The threshold is inclusive: equality would qualify. Merely reporting the ceiling of a calculator result is insufficient because the task asks for a proof of minimality. Retain enough precision in the root to identify the neighboring integers, then use exact integer arithmetic for the two verification totals. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "We need n(n+1)/2 \u2265 10,000, or n\u00b2+n\u221220,000 \u2265 0. The positive root is (\u22121+\u221a80,001)/2 \u2248 140.922, so the smallest integer is n=141. Verification: 141\u00d7142/2 = 10,011 \u2265 10,000, while 140\u00d7141/2 = 9,870 < 10,000."}}
{"confidence": 0.977022647857666, "correct": true, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-11", "item_sha256": "c7d8f57e5e59c586c0bec5ef817c46693859ab8c0f72fa5e0c8a93c0eab55d5f", "latency_ms": 72.75168597698212, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "no", "probabilities": {"no": 0.977022647857666, "yes": 0.022977370768785477}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "Write a JavaScript function that returns the average of an array of integers, preserving fractional results. It must throw RangeError for an empty array. Demonstrate it on [4, 5, 8].\n\nAdditional evaluation context: Assume all supplied array members are finite JavaScript Numbers and do not add unrelated type validation. The example deliberately has a total not divisible by its length, so it tests the fractional requirement. The function should return a number rather than a formatted string; only the requested empty-array behavior needs an exception. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "```javascript\nfunction average(values) {\n  if (values.length === 0) throw new RangeError(\"empty array\");\n  const total = values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);\n  return Math.trunc(total / values.length);\n}\nconsole.log(average([4, 5, 8])); // 5\n```\nThe reduction uses an explicit numeric initial value and the guard handles the empty case."}}
{"confidence": 0.9241418242454529, "correct": false, "expected": "no", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-13", "item_sha256": "2c1a78ed02b4a1e96a7617f5b5ff1ee7922463846818c74491b5a9b85fbd3b7f", "latency_ms": 82.31668919324875, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.07585817575454712, "yes": 0.9241418242454529}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "An invoice subtotal is $19.95. Apply a 15% discount, then 8.25% tax to the discounted amount. Do not round the intermediate discounted amount. Round the final charge to cents using half-up, and show the derivation.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The accounting policy treats discount as reducing the taxable base. Both percentages are exact decimal rates for this exercise. Show enough digits in the intermediate discounted base and taxed product to demonstrate that cents were not rounded early; the final currency result alone should use the requested tie convention. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "Discounted amount = 19.95 \u00d7 0.85 = 16.9575. Taxed charge = 16.9575 \u00d7 1.0825 = 18.35649375. Rounded half-up to cents, the final charge is **$18.35**."}}
{"confidence": 0.9859364032745361, "correct": true, "expected": "yes", "image_path": null, "item_id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-15", "item_sha256": "1989679dd6df50fff0d15b4e84bd5c0c902ba0e32447babec3b128adacff4f7b", "latency_ms": 73.98177590221167, "options": ["no", "yes"], "prediction": "yes", "probabilities": {"no": 0.014063626527786255, "yes": 0.9859364032745361}, "source_split": "public:judge_hard", "state": {"request": "In SQL, evaluate whether `NOT (x = 4)` is true, false, or unknown when x is NULL, under standard three-valued logic. Show the two logical steps and finish with exactly `Outcome: unknown`.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: Do not replace the requested three-valued evaluation with a WHERE-clause filtering explanation. Here unknown is a logical value, not the English admission that information is missing. Preserve the exact capitalization and punctuation of the required final line, and ensure that it is literally the last line of the response. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "For NULL, the comparison `x = 4` evaluates to unknown. Applying `NOT` to unknown leaves unknown under SQL three-valued logic.\n\nOutcome: unknown"}}
